/*
 * OCR A A-Level Chemistry (H432) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * M1: 25 questions, M2-M6: 50 each; ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written as one part of a real H432 question, not
 * as a revision flashcard. Papers 1 and 2 (100 marks, 2 h 15) are 15 marks of
 * multiple choice (those live in chemistry-ocr-a.js) and 85 marks of structured
 * questions; Paper 3 Unified chemistry (70 marks) is all structured and synoptic.
 * Each structured question opens with a stem — a reaction, a set of results, a
 * student's method, an unknown compound — and the parts hang off it. So almost every
 * item here carries a stem, and practical skills (Module 1) are assessed in context
 * through the PAGs, as on the papers.
 *
 * TARIFFS. H432 parts are worth 1 to 6 marks, most of them 1–3, with a few 6-mark
 * Level of Response parts (6*) per paper.
 *   MIX PER 50-ITEM MODULE: 10 x 1, 14 x 2, 12 x 3, 7 x 4, 3 x 5, 4 x 6.
 *   MODULE 1 (25 items):     5 x 1,  7 x 2,  6 x 3, 4 x 4, 1 x 5, 2 x 6.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES follow OCR's layout: one marking point per line ending in a tick,
 * then ALLOW / IGNORE / DO NOT ALLOW / ECF guidance. The 6* items list indicative
 * scientific points by strand and the Level 3/2/1 descriptors OCR applies; the
 * indicative points are not a checklist.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_OCR_A_PRACTICE = {
  'M1': {
    name: 'Development of Practical Skills',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student titrates 25.0 cm³ of aqueous ethanoic acid with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide. The sodium hydroxide is added from a burette and phenolphthalein is used as the indicator.\n\nState the colour change in the conical flask at the end point.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `colourless to (pale) pink ✓\nALLOW colourless to purple / magenta\nDO NOT ALLOW pink to colourless (wrong direction)\nDO NOT ALLOW clear for colourless` },
      { q: `As part of a research task, a class compares its values for the enthalpy change of neutralisation of hydrochloric acid by sodium hydroxide with a literature value of −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹. Three groups each carried out the experiment three times. (data written for this practice question)\n\nGroup A: −56.8, −57.5, −56.1 kJ mol⁻¹\nGroup B: −52.2, −52.3, −52.1 kJ mol⁻¹\nGroup C: −57.0, −57.2, −56.9 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nIdentify the group whose results are precise but not accurate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Group B ✓\n(values very close to each other but all well away from −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹)\nIGNORE explanation` },
      { q: `A student adds dilute nitric acid followed by aqueous silver nitrate to a solution of an unknown sodium halide. A cream precipitate forms. The student thinks the halide is bromide.\n\nState the observation when concentrated aqueous ammonia is then added to the cream precipitate, if the student is correct.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `precipitate dissolves / colourless solution forms ✓\nALLOW precipitate disappears\nDO NOT ALLOW precipitate partially dissolves\nIGNORE references to dilute ammonia` },
      { q: `A student prepares aspirin and recrystallises the crude product from a mixture of ethanol and water. The dried crystals melt over the range 128–132 °C. The literature melting point of aspirin is 135 °C.\n\nState what the melting point range shows about the student's product.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `the product is impure / still contains impurities ✓\n(impurities lower the melting point and broaden the range)\nALLOW not pure aspirin\nIGNORE wet` },
      { q: `A student sets up an electrochemical cell from a Cu²⁺(aq)/Cu(s) half-cell and an Ag⁺(aq)/Ag(s) half-cell. The Ag⁺ half-cell contains aqueous silver nitrate. The salt bridge is a strip of filter paper soaked in saturated potassium nitrate.\n\nExplain why the salt bridge is not soaked in potassium chloride.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cl⁻ ions would react with Ag⁺ ions to form a precipitate of AgCl ✓\nALLOW Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl\nALLOW chloride would remove Ag⁺ / change [Ag⁺] by precipitation\nIGNORE potassium chloride reacts with the electrodes` },
      { q: `A student determines the molar volume of hydrogen by reacting magnesium ribbon with excess dilute hydrochloric acid and collecting the gas in a 100 cm³ gas syringe.\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nThe mass of magnesium is found by difference on a balance that reads to 0.001 g; each reading has an uncertainty of ±0.0005 g. The mass of magnesium used is 0.050 g. The gas syringe has an uncertainty of ±0.5 cm³ and the volume of hydrogen collected is 48.0 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the mass of magnesium and the percentage uncertainty in the volume of hydrogen.\nGive both answers to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass: (2 × 0.0005 / 0.050) × 100 = 2.0% ✓\nvolume: (0.5 / 48.0) × 100 = 1.0% ✓\nALLOW 1.04% for volume\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.0% for mass (only one reading counted)` },
      { q: `A student makes a standard solution of sodium carbonate. The student weighs about 2.65 g of anhydrous Na₂CO₃ into a beaker, dissolves it in about 100 cm³ of deionised water, and then transfers the solution to a 250.0 cm³ volumetric flask.\n\nDescribe two further steps the student should take so that the concentration of the solution in the volumetric flask is accurately known.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `rinse the beaker (and glass rod / funnel) with deionised water and add the washings to the volumetric flask ✓\nmake up to the graduation mark with deionised water so the bottom of the meniscus is on the line (at eye level) ✓\nALLOW stopper and invert / shake the flask to mix thoroughly for either mark\nALLOW reweigh the weighing boat and use the mass by difference\nIGNORE wear goggles` },
      { q: `A student prepares 1-bromobutane by heating butan-1-ol with sodium bromide and concentrated sulfuric acid under reflux in a pear-shaped flask.\n\nExplain two safety precautions the student should take when setting up and heating the reflux apparatus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any two from:\nadd anti-bumping granules so the mixture boils smoothly / to prevent violent boiling ✓\ndo not put a stopper in the top of the condenser so pressure does not build up (and the apparatus does not explode) ✓\nheat with an electric heating mantle / water bath rather than a Bunsen flame because the organic compounds are flammable ✓\nconnect water to the bottom inlet of the condenser so the condenser stays full and vapour condenses / does not escape ✓\nwear gloves because concentrated sulfuric acid is corrosive ✓\nEach mark needs the precaution AND the reason` },
      { q: `A student has three unlabelled bottles, each containing one isomer of C₄H₁₀O: butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol. A sample of each is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). (data written for this practice question)\n\nBottle A: orange to green\nBottle B: orange to green\nBottle C: stays orange\n\nDeduce which bottle contains 2-methylpropan-2-ol and explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `C (is 2-methylpropan-2-ol) ✓\nit is a tertiary alcohol / there is no hydrogen atom on the carbon bonded to the OH group, so it is not oxidised ✓\nALLOW tertiary alcohols are not oxidised for second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW tertiary alcohols do not react with acid` },
      { q: `A student uses an iodine clock reaction to find initial rates. Hydrogen peroxide is added to a mixture of acidified potassium iodide, a small fixed amount of sodium thiosulfate and starch. The student records the time, t, for the blue-black colour to appear, and uses 1/t as a measure of the initial rate.\n\nH₂O₂(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → I₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\nI₂(aq) + 2S₂O₃²⁻(aq) → 2I⁻(aq) + S₄O₆²⁻(aq)\n\nExplain why 1/t can be used as a measure of the initial rate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the blue-black colour appears when all the thiosulfate has been used up, so the same (fixed) amount of I₂ has formed in each experiment ✓\nrate = amount formed ÷ time, so rate ∝ 1/t (and only a small amount reacts, so the rate is approximately the initial rate) ✓\nALLOW same amount of H₂O₂ / I⁻ reacted each time` },
      { q: `A student calibrates a pH meter and then measures the pH of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, at 25 °C. The pH is 2.88.\n\nCalculate the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of ethanoic acid.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 10⁻²·⁸⁸ = 1.32 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ ✓\nKa = [H⁺]² / [CH₃COOH] = (1.32 × 10⁻³)² / 0.100 = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ (mol dm⁻³) ✓\nALLOW 1.76 × 10⁻⁵ (using 0.100 − [H⁺])\nALLOW 1.73 × 10⁻⁵ to 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ from rounding\nECF from incorrect [H⁺]\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `A student builds a cell from a Zn²⁺(aq)/Zn(s) half-cell and a Cu²⁺(aq)/Cu(s) half-cell at 298 K, connected by a salt bridge and a high-resistance voltmeter. The Zn²⁺ solution is 1.00 mol dm⁻³ but the student mistakenly uses 0.100 mol dm⁻³ Cu²⁺(aq). The measured cell potential is 1.07 V; the standard cell potential calculated from the Data Sheet is 1.10 V.\n\nZn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn(s)   E⦵ = −0.76 V\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu(s)   E⦵ = +0.34 V\n\nExplain why the measured cell potential is less than 1.10 V.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `lower [Cu²⁺] shifts the Cu²⁺/Cu equilibrium to the left (Cu²⁺ less readily reduced) ✓\nso the Cu²⁺/Cu electrode potential is less positive (than +0.34 V) and the difference between the two electrode potentials is smaller ✓\nALLOW Cu half-cell potential decreases\nDO NOT ALLOW answers based on heat loss / resistance of wires\nIGNORE non-standard conditions without explanation` },
      { q: `A student titrates 25.0 cm³ of dilute sulfuric acid with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide from a burette. (data written for this practice question)\n\nTitration | Final reading / cm³ | Initial reading / cm³ | Titre / cm³\nRough | 24.30 | 0.50 | 23.80\n1 | 23.65 | 0.20 | 23.45\n2 | 24.20 | 1.05 | 23.15\n3 | 23.75 | 0.35 | 23.40\n\nH₂SO₄(aq) + 2NaOH(aq) → Na₂SO₄(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of the sulfuric acid.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean titre from concordant titres 1 and 3 = (23.45 + 23.40)/2 = 23.43 cm³ ✓\nn(NaOH) = 0.100 × 23.43/1000 = 2.343 × 10⁻³ mol AND n(H₂SO₄) = 2.343 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 1.171 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\n[H₂SO₄] = 1.171 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.0469 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nALLOW mean 23.425 cm³\nDO NOT ALLOW use of the rough titre or titre 2 in the mean for the first mark\nECF from incorrect mean titre or n(NaOH)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student adds 50.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid to 50.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide in a polystyrene cup. The initial temperature of both solutions is 21.4 °C and the maximum temperature reached is 28.0 °C.\n\nAssume the density of the mixture is 1.00 g cm⁻³ and its specific heat capacity is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of neutralisation, ΔneutH, in kJ mol⁻¹.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 100.0 × 4.18 × 6.6 = 2758.8 J ✓\nn(H₂O formed) = 1.00 × 50.0/1000 = 0.0500 mol ✓\nΔneutH = −2758.8 / (0.0500 × 1000) = −55.2 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nThird mark needs the negative sign and 3 s.f.\nDO NOT ALLOW m = 50.0 g (gives −27.6 kJ mol⁻¹ — scores max 2 by ECF)\nECF from incorrect q or n\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student follows the reaction between bromine and excess methanoic acid using a colorimeter, converting each absorbance reading to a concentration of bromine. (data written for this practice question)\n\nBr₂(aq) + HCOOH(aq) → 2HBr(aq) + CO₂(g)\n\nTime / s | [Br₂] / mol dm⁻³\n0 | 0.0100\n60 | 0.00707\n120 | 0.00500\n180 | 0.00354\n240 | 0.00250\n300 | 0.00177\n360 | 0.00125\n\nShow that the reaction is first order with respect to bromine and calculate the rate constant, k.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures, with units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `half-life is 120 s each time (0.0100 → 0.00500 → 0.00250 → 0.00125 at 120 s intervals) ✓\nconstant half-life, so first order with respect to Br₂ ✓\nk = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693/120 = 5.78 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ ✓\nThird mark needs value AND unit s⁻¹\nALLOW 5.77 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹\nALLOW alternative: rate ÷ [Br₂] is constant for second mark if justified with data` },
      { q: `A student prepares aspirin, C₉H₈O₄, by reacting 2.00 g of 2-hydroxybenzoic acid, C₇H₆O₃, with excess ethanoic anhydride. One mole of 2-hydroxybenzoic acid forms one mole of aspirin. After recrystallisation from hot water, the dry purified aspirin has a mass of 1.84 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of aspirin, and suggest one reason why some product is lost during recrystallisation.\nGive your percentage yield to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(C₇H₆O₃) = 2.00/138.0 = 0.01449 mol (= theoretical n(aspirin)) ✓\n% yield = 1.84 / (0.01449 × 180.0) × 100 = 1.84/2.609 × 100 = 70.5% ✓\nsome aspirin stays dissolved in the solvent / filtrate (even when cold) ✓\nALLOW crystals left on the filter paper / in the flask / lost during transfer\nALLOW too much hot solvent used\nECF from incorrect n\nCorrect answer with no working scores first two marks` },
      { q: `A student heats a sample of hydrated magnesium sulfate, MgSO₄•xH₂O, in a crucible until the mass is constant. (data written for this practice question)\n\nMass of empty crucible = 15.212 g\nMass of crucible + hydrated salt = 17.676 g\nMass of crucible + anhydrous salt = 16.416 g\n\nDetermine the value of x.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `mass MgSO₄ = 1.204 g AND mass H₂O = 1.260 g ✓\nn(MgSO₄) = 1.204/120.4 = 0.0100 mol AND n(H₂O) = 1.260/18.0 = 0.0700 mol ✓\nx = 0.0700/0.0100 = 7 ✓\nALLOW x = 7 from correct M of hydrated salt (2.464/0.0100 = 246.4)\nECF from incorrect masses\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student repeats an iodine clock experiment at five different temperatures and finds the rate constant, k, at each. The student plots ln k against 1/T. The best-fit straight line passes through the points (3.20 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, −3.10) and (3.50 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, −5.14). (data written for this practice question)\n\nThe Arrhenius equation can be written as ln k = −Ea/RT + ln A.\n\nDetermine the activation energy, Ea, in kJ mol⁻¹.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `gradient = (−5.14 − (−3.10)) / (3.50 × 10⁻³ − 3.20 × 10⁻³) = −6800 (K) ✓\ngradient = −Ea/R, so Ea = 6800 × 8.314 = 56 535 J mol⁻¹ ✓\nEa = 56.5 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW −56.5 kJ mol⁻¹ for 2 marks only\nECF from incorrect gradient\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student uses a 25.0 cm³ pipette to transfer hydrochloric acid into a conical flask and titrates it with sodium hydroxide from a 50.00 cm³ burette. The mean titre is 12.50 cm³.\n\nThe pipette has an uncertainty of ±0.06 cm³. Each burette reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the pipette volume and in the titre. Then suggest one change to the procedure, using the same apparatus, that would reduce the percentage uncertainty in the titre, and explain why it works.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pipette: (0.06/25.0) × 100 = 0.24% ✓\ntitre: (2 × 0.05/12.50) × 100 = 0.80% ✓\nuse a more dilute sodium hydroxide solution (e.g. half the concentration) ✓\nthe titre would be larger (about 25 cm³), so the same absolute uncertainty is a smaller percentage (about 0.40%) ✓\nALLOW use a more concentrated acid / pipette 50 cm³ of acid into the flask for third mark\nDO NOT ALLOW use a more precise burette (not the same apparatus)\nDO NOT ALLOW repeat the titration (does not reduce the uncertainty of a reading)` },
      { q: `A student is given a solution that contains sodium carbonate, sodium sulfate and sodium chloride. The student wants to show that all three anions are present by carrying out tests, one after another, on a single sample of the solution.\n\nExplain the order in which the tests must be carried out and the choice of reagents, giving the observation for each positive test.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `order: carbonate, then sulfate, then halide ✓\ncarbonate first: add dilute nitric acid until no more effervescence; gas turns limewater milky; this removes CO₃²⁻, which would otherwise give a white precipitate (BaCO₃ / Ag₂CO₃) in later tests ✓\nsulfate next: add Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) — white precipitate (BaSO₄); done before halide test because Ag₂SO₄ can also precipitate with silver nitrate ✓\nhalide: add AgNO₃(aq) to the (filtered) solution — white precipitate (AgCl); barium nitrate and nitric acid used (not BaCl₂ or HCl) so no chloride ions are added ✓\nALLOW BaCl₂ for sulfate test only if halide test is on a separate sample (not the case here)\nDO NOT ALLOW hydrochloric acid or sulfuric acid in the carbonate test` },
      { q: `A student investigates the rate of the reaction between hydrogen peroxide and iodide ions in acid using the initial rates method. (data written for this practice question)\n\nH₂O₂(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → I₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nExpt | [H₂O₂] / mol dm⁻³ | [I⁻] / mol dm⁻³ | [H⁺] / mol dm⁻³ | Initial rate / mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n1 | 0.0100 | 0.0100 | 0.100 | 1.75 × 10⁻⁶\n2 | 0.0200 | 0.0100 | 0.100 | 3.50 × 10⁻⁶\n3 | 0.0100 | 0.0200 | 0.100 | 3.50 × 10⁻⁶\n4 | 0.0100 | 0.0100 | 0.200 | 1.75 × 10⁻⁶\n\nDetermine the rate equation and calculate the rate constant, k, with units.\nGive k to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `first order with respect to H₂O₂ (expts 1 and 2: [H₂O₂] doubles, rate doubles) ✓\nfirst order with respect to I⁻ AND zero order with respect to H⁺ (expts 1 and 3; expts 1 and 4) ✓\nrate = k[H₂O₂][I⁻] AND k = 1.75 × 10⁻⁶ / (0.0100 × 0.0100) = 0.0175 ✓\nunits: dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 1.75 × 10⁻² dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹\nALLOW mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹\nECF for k and units from an incorrect rate equation` },
      { q: `A student prepares cyclohexene by heating cyclohexanol with concentrated phosphoric acid and distilling off the product as it forms. The distillate is cloudy and contains two layers: impure cyclohexene and an aqueous layer containing some acid.\n\nCyclohexene: boiling point 83 °C, density 0.81 g cm⁻³, immiscible with water.\n\nDescribe how the student should obtain a pure, dry sample of cyclohexene from the distillate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `pour the distillate into a separating funnel, add aqueous sodium carbonate / sodium hydrogencarbonate to neutralise the acid (releasing the pressure / tap regularly) ✓\nrun off and discard the lower aqueous layer, keeping the upper (organic) cyclohexene layer ✓\nadd an anhydrous drying agent such as anhydrous CaCl₂ / MgSO₄ / Na₂SO₄, swirl until the liquid is clear, then decant / filter off the drying agent ✓\nredistil and collect the fraction boiling at about 83 °C (e.g. 81–85 °C) ✓\nALLOW wash with water / saturated NaCl(aq) for first mark\nDO NOT ALLOW cyclohexene is the lower layer` },
      { q: `A student measures the enthalpy change for the reaction of zinc with copper(II) sulfate. The student records the temperature of 50.0 cm³ of 0.500 mol dm⁻³ CuSO₄(aq) in a polystyrene cup every 30 s for 3 minutes, adds an excess of zinc powder at 3.0 minutes, stirs, and continues to record the temperature every 30 s until 10 minutes. The temperature before adding zinc is steady at 21.0 °C. The student plots temperature against time and extrapolates the cooling line back to 3.0 minutes, giving a temperature of 45.0 °C.\n\nZn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)\n\nAssume the solution has density 1.00 g cm⁻³ and specific heat capacity 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of reaction, ΔrH, in kJ mol⁻¹, to 3 significant figures. Explain why the student extrapolates the cooling line rather than using the highest temperature recorded.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 24.0 = 5016 J ✓\nn(CuSO₄) = 0.500 × 50.0/1000 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nΔrH = −5016 / (0.0250 × 1000) = −201 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nthe reaction takes time to go to completion, and heat is lost to the surroundings during this time (so the highest recorded temperature is too low) ✓\nextrapolating back to the time of mixing gives the temperature rise that would occur if the reaction were instantaneous / with no heat loss ✓\nThird mark needs negative sign and 3 s.f.\nALLOW −200.6 kJ mol⁻¹\nDO NOT ALLOW mass including the zinc\nECF from incorrect q or n` },
      { q: `A student is given a solution of a weak monobasic acid, HA, of unknown concentration, 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide, a pH meter, buffer solutions of pH 4.00, 7.00 and 10.00, and standard laboratory glassware.\n\nDescribe how the student could carry out an experiment to determine the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of HA, and explain how Ka is obtained from the results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• calibrate the pH meter using the pH 4.00 and 7.00 (and 10.00) buffers, rinsing the electrode with deionised water between solutions\n• pipette 25.0 cm³ of HA into a beaker / conical flask; fill a burette with NaOH\n• add NaOH in small portions (e.g. 1.00 cm³), stir / swirl, record pH after each addition\n• add smaller portions (e.g. 0.10–0.20 cm³) near the equivalence point; continue well past it\n• keep temperature constant because Ka depends on temperature\n2. Processing\n• plot pH (y-axis) against volume of NaOH added (x-axis)\n• equivalence volume is at the centre of the vertical section of the curve\n• find half the equivalence volume and read the pH at that volume from the curve\n3. Determining Ka\n• at half-neutralisation [HA] = [A⁻], so Ka = [H⁺] and pH = pKa\n• Ka = 10⁻ᵖᴴ at the half-equivalence point\n• (concentration of HA, if needed, from equivalence volume: n(HA) = n(NaOH))\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student has three unlabelled bottles. Each contains one of the following isomers of C₄H₈O:\n\nbutanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO\nbutanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃\nbut-2-en-1-ol, CH₃CH=CHCH₂OH\n\nDescribe a series of chemical tests the student could carry out to identify the compound in each bottle. Include reagents, observations and how the results distinguish the three compounds.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Tests and reagents\n• 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP / Brady's reagent) to test for a carbonyl group\n• Tollens' reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate), warmed in a water bath, to test for an aldehyde\n• bromine water to test for C=C\n• ALLOW acidified potassium dichromate(VI), warm, as an alternative to Tollens' (butanal and but-2-en-1-ol turn orange to green, butanone stays orange)\n2. Observations\n• butanal: orange / yellow precipitate with 2,4-DNP; silver mirror with Tollens'; bromine water stays orange\n• butanone: orange / yellow precipitate with 2,4-DNP; no change with Tollens'; bromine water stays orange\n• but-2-en-1-ol: no precipitate with 2,4-DNP; bromine water decolourised (orange to colourless)\n3. Interpretation and practical detail\n• 2,4-DNP precipitate shows C=O present in butanal and butanone but not in the alcohol\n• only the aldehyde is oxidised by Tollens' (Ag⁺ reduced to Ag)\n• decolourisation of bromine water shows C=C in but-2-en-1-ol\n• warm Tollens' in a hot water bath, not a flame, because the compounds are flammable; use small samples in test tubes\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'M2': {
    name: 'Foundations in Chemistry',
    questions: [
      { q: `Chromium is used to make stainless steel. A chromium atom has 24 electrons.\n\nWrite the full electron configuration of a chromium atom.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹ ✓\nALLOW 4s¹ written before 3d⁵\nDO NOT ALLOW [Ar] shorthand\nDO NOT ALLOW 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `Potassium manganate(VII), KMnO₄, is used as an oxidising agent in titrations.\n\nDeduce the oxidation number of manganese in KMnO₄.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `+7 ✓\nALLOW 7+ OR +VII\nDO NOT ALLOW Mn⁷⁺ (charge, not oxidation number)` },
      { q: `A student adds iron filings to dilute sulfuric acid. Hydrogen gas is given off and a pale green solution of FeSO₄ forms.\n\nName FeSO₄, using a Roman numeral in your answer.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `iron(II) sulfate ✓\nALLOW iron(II) sulfate(VI)\nDO NOT ALLOW iron(2) sulfate OR iron sulfate` },
      { q: `Sulfur hexafluoride, SF₆, is an unreactive gas used as an electrical insulator in high-voltage switchgear.\n\nState the shape of an SF₆ molecule.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `octahedral ✓\nIGNORE bond angle\nDO NOT ALLOW hexagonal` },
      { q: `A student carries out an acid–base titration (PAG2) using a 50 cm³ burette graduated in 0.1 cm³ divisions.\n\nState the uncertainty in a single reading of this burette.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `±0.05 cm³ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW ±0.1 cm³ (that is the uncertainty in a titre from two readings)` },
      { q: `Dilute nitric acid is neutralised by aqueous potassium hydroxide.\n\nWrite the ionic equation for this neutralisation. Include state symbols.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l) ✓\nALLOW H₃O⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → 2H₂O(l)\nState symbols required for the mark` },
      { q: `Ammonium sulfate is used as a nitrogen fertiliser. It contains the NH₄⁺ ion and the SO₄²⁻ ion.\n\nWrite the formula of ammonium sulfate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `(NH₄)₂SO₄ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW NH₄SO₄ OR NH₄₂SO₄` },
      { q: `The electron structure of an atom can be described in terms of shells (principal energy levels), sub-shells and orbitals. The third shell (n = 3) contains s, p and d sub-shells.\n\nState the maximum number of electrons that the third shell can hold.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `18 ✓` },
      { q: `Liquid ammonia, NH₃, boils at −33 °C, much higher than phosphine, PH₃, which boils at −88 °C.\n\nIdentify the strongest type of intermolecular force between NH₃ molecules in liquid ammonia.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `hydrogen bonding ✓\nALLOW hydrogen bonds\nDO NOT ALLOW 'hydrogen' alone OR permanent dipole–dipole` },
      { q: `Hydrochloric acid, HCl(aq), is described as a strong acid, whereas ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH(aq), is a weak acid.\n\nState what is meant by the term strong acid.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `An acid that completely dissociates (in water / in solution) ✓\nALLOW fully ionises OR releases all its H⁺ ions\nIGNORE references to concentration or pH\nDO NOT ALLOW 'concentrated'` },
      { q: `A sample of magnesium was analysed in a mass spectrometer. The isotopic composition is shown below.\n\nIsotope | ²⁴Mg | ²⁵Mg | ²⁶Mg\nAbundance / % | 78.99 | 10.00 | 11.01\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass, Ar, of this sample of magnesium. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(24 × 78.99) + (25 × 10.00) + (26 × 11.01) = 2432.02 ✓\nAr = 2432.02 / 100 = 24.32 ✓ (to 2 d.p.)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 24.3 OR 24.320 (wrong number of decimal places)` },
      { q: `Sulfur-34 is a stable isotope of sulfur. Sulfur forms sulfide ions, S²⁻, in compounds such as sodium sulfide.\n\nDeduce the numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons in a ³⁴S²⁻ ion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `protons: 16 AND neutrons: 18 ✓\nelectrons: 18 ✓` },
      { q: `Magnesium ribbon reacts with dilute sulfuric acid. Effervescence is seen and a colourless solution of magnesium sulfate forms.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction. Include state symbols.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Mg + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂ ✓ (correct species and balanced)\nMg(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → MgSO₄(aq) + H₂(g) ✓ (state symbols)\nState symbol mark is dependent on correct species\nALLOW ionic equation Mg(s) + 2H⁺(aq) → Mg²⁺(aq) + H₂(g) for both marks` },
      { q: `In a blast furnace, iron is extracted from iron(III) oxide by reduction with carbon monoxide:\n\nFe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂\n\nCalculate the percentage atom economy for the formation of iron in this process. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mr of reactants = 159.6 + (3 × 28.0) = 243.6 ✓\natom economy = (2 × 55.8) / 243.6 × 100 = 45.8% ✓\nALLOW use of products: 111.6 / (111.6 + 132.0) × 100\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks\nALLOW 45.8 to 45.9 from rounding of Ar values` },
      { q: `Vinegar contains ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH. A 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of ethanoic acid has a much higher pH than 0.100 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid.\n\nExplain why ethanoic acid is classed as a weak acid. Include an equation in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(Ethanoic acid) partially dissociates (in water) ✓\nCH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ ✓\nEquilibrium sign required for the equation mark\nALLOW CH₃COOH + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H₃O⁺\nALLOW 'only a small proportion of molecules release H⁺'` },
      { q: `Zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid:\n\nZn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nExplain, in terms of oxidation numbers, why this is a redox reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Zn is oxidised: oxidation number increases from 0 to +2 ✓\nH is reduced: oxidation number decreases from +1 to 0 ✓\nBoth changes needed with the correct numbers for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 'Zn oxidised and H reduced' with no or incorrect oxidation numbers\nIGNORE Cl: oxidation number −1, unchanged` },
      { q: `A technician dissolves 4.00 g of sodium hydroxide, NaOH, in distilled water and makes the solution up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask.\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of the sodium hydroxide solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(NaOH) = 4.00 / 40.0 = 0.100 mol ✓\nconcentration = 0.100 / 0.2500 = 0.400 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nECF from incorrect n(NaOH)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `In a moles determination (PAG1), a student weighs a sample of magnesium by difference on a two-decimal-place balance. Each balance reading has an uncertainty of ±0.005 g. The mass of magnesium used was 0.24 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the mass of magnesium. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `uncertainty = 2 × 0.005 = 0.01 g (two readings) ✓\npercentage uncertainty = 0.01 / 0.24 × 100 = 4.2% ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 2.1% (only one reading used)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide is a linear molecule, O=C=O. Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon.\n\nExplain why carbon dioxide is a non-polar molecule even though it contains polar bonds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Each C=O bond is polar / has a dipole (Cδ+ and Oδ−) ✓\nThe molecule is symmetrical / linear so the dipoles act in opposite directions and cancel ✓\nALLOW 'dipoles cancel' for the second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW 'charges cancel' OR 'bonds cancel'` },
      { q: `Phosphine, PH₃, is a toxic gas. Phosphorus is in Group 15 and forms three covalent bonds to hydrogen in PH₃.\n\nPredict the shape of a PH₃ molecule and the H–P–H bond angle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `(trigonal) pyramidal ✓\n107° ✓\nALLOW any bond angle from 90° to 107°\nALLOW 'pyramid'\nDO NOT ALLOW 'trigonal planar' OR 'tetrahedral'\n(The actual angle in PH₃ is about 93.5°; OCR accepts the electron-pair repulsion prediction of 107°.)` },
      { q: `Ammonia reacts with hydrogen chloride to form ammonium chloride, which contains the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺.\n\nDraw a 'dot-and-cross' diagram of the NH₄⁺ ion. Show outer electrons only and identify the dative covalent bond.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `N with four bonding pairs to four H atoms; three pairs shown as dot-and-cross shared pairs ✓\nOne pair with both electrons from N (e.g. two dots or two crosses) identified as the dative bond, AND overall charge + shown (e.g. square brackets with +) ✓\nALLOW the dative bond shown as an arrow from N to H alongside the diagram\nIGNORE inner shell electrons if shown correctly` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride does not conduct electricity when solid but does conduct when molten.\n\nExplain this difference in electrical conductivity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Solid: ions are fixed in position in the (giant ionic) lattice / cannot move ✓\nMolten: ions are free to move (and carry charge) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 'electrons move' OR 'delocalised electrons' — CON for that mark\nIGNORE 'molecules' only if ions are also stated` },
      { q: `Water molecules in liquid water are held together by hydrogen bonds.\n\nDraw a labelled diagram to show a hydrogen bond between two water molecules. Include relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Two H₂O molecules with dipoles shown on at least one O–H bond (Oδ− and Hδ+) ✓\nHydrogen bond drawn (dashed/dotted line, labelled) from a lone pair on the O of one molecule to an Hδ+ of the other molecule ✓\nThe H-bond must clearly start from a lone pair on O\nALLOW the H…O–H arrangement drawn approximately linear` },
      { q: `A student carried out an acid–base titration (PAG2). The results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nTitration | Rough | 1 | 2 | 3\nFinal reading / cm³ | 24.50 | 47.95 | 24.20 | 48.10\nInitial reading / cm³ | 0.00 | 24.10 | 0.00 | 24.20\nTitre / cm³ | 24.50 | 23.85 | 24.20 | 23.90\n\nCalculate the mean titre the student should use in further calculations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Uses concordant titres only (within 0.10 cm³): titres 1 and 3, 23.85 and 23.90 ✓\nmean titre = (23.85 + 23.90) / 2 = 23.88 cm³ ✓\nALLOW 23.875 cm³\nDO NOT ALLOW a mean that includes the rough titre OR titre 2 (23.98 OR 24.03 OR 24.10 scores 0)` },
      { q: `A student heated a sample of hydrated magnesium sulfate, MgSO₄·xH₂O, in a crucible to determine the value of x (PAG1). The student's results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nMass of empty crucible / g: 15.32\nMass of crucible + hydrated salt / g: 17.78\nMass of crucible + residue after heating to constant mass / g: 16.52\n\nCalculate the value of x. Give your answer as a whole number.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass MgSO₄ = 1.20 g AND mass H₂O = 1.26 g ✓\nn(MgSO₄) = 1.20 / 120.4 = 9.97 × 10⁻³ mol AND n(H₂O) = 1.26 / 18.0 = 0.0700 mol ✓\nx = 0.0700 / 9.97 × 10⁻³ = 7.02, so x = 7 ✓\nECF from incorrect masses or moles\nCorrect answer with no working scores 1 mark only (x = 7 must be supported)` },
      { q: `When calcium carbonate is heated strongly it decomposes:\n\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\n\nA student completely decomposes 0.500 g of CaCO₃.\n\nCalculate the volume, in cm³, of CO₂ produced at 298 K and 100 kPa. Use the ideal gas equation. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(CO₂) = n(CaCO₃) = 0.500 / 100.1 = 4.995 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nV = nRT / p = (4.995 × 10⁻³ × 8.314 × 298) / (100 × 10³) = 1.24 × 10⁻⁴ m³ ✓\nV = 124 cm³ ✓ (3 s.f.)\nECF throughout\nALLOW 1 mark for the unit conversion error giving 0.124 OR 124 000\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A white solid oxide of phosphorus contains 43.7% phosphorus by mass. Its relative molecular mass is 284.\n\nDetermine the empirical formula and the molecular formula of the oxide. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `P: 43.7 / 31.0 = 1.41 AND O: 56.3 / 16.0 = 3.52 ✓\nratio 1 : 2.5, so empirical formula = P₂O₅ ✓\nMr(P₂O₅) = 142; 284 / 142 = 2, so molecular formula = P₄O₁₀ ✓\nECF for the molecular formula from an incorrect empirical formula\nALLOW P₂O₅ ✓✓ for empirical formula with no working` },
      { q: `A student prepares hydrated copper(II) sulfate crystals by reacting copper(II) oxide with excess dilute sulfuric acid, filtering and crystallising:\n\nCuO + H₂SO₄ + 4H₂O → CuSO₄·5H₂O\n\nThe student starts with 2.00 g of CuO and obtains 5.21 g of CuSO₄·5H₂O.\n\nCalculate the percentage yield. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(CuO) = 2.00 / 79.5 = 0.02516 mol ✓\ntheoretical mass CuSO₄·5H₂O = 0.02516 × 249.6 = 6.28 g ✓\npercentage yield = 5.21 / 6.28 × 100 = 83.0% ✓\nALLOW via moles: n(product) = 5.21 / 249.6 = 0.02087 mol; 0.02087 / 0.02516 × 100\nECF from incorrect Mr values\nALLOW 82.9% to 83.0% from rounding` },
      { q: `The water molecule, H₂O, has an H–O–H bond angle of 104.5°.\n\nExplain why a water molecule has this shape and bond angle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `O has two bonding pairs and two lone pairs (of electrons) ✓\nElectron pairs repel to be as far apart as possible ✓\nLone pairs repel more than bonding pairs, so the angle is reduced from 109.5° (by about 2.5° per lone pair) ✓\nALLOW 'non-linear' OR 'bent' for the shape\nDO NOT ALLOW 'atoms repel' OR 'bonds repel' for the second mark` },
      { q: `Water, H₂O, boils at 100 °C whereas hydrogen sulfide, H₂S, which has a greater molar mass, boils at −60 °C.\n\nExplain this difference in boiling points.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `H₂O has hydrogen bonding (between molecules) ✓\nH₂S has (permanent) dipole–dipole and London forces only / no hydrogen bonding (S not electronegative enough) ✓\nHydrogen bonds are stronger, so more energy is needed to overcome the intermolecular forces in H₂O ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 'breaking covalent bonds' — CON for the third mark\nALLOW 'induced dipole–dipole interactions' for London forces` },
      { q: `Aluminium powder reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to form aluminium chloride solution and hydrogen gas.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction and use oxidation numbers to identify the species oxidised and the species reduced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2Al + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂ ✓\nAl oxidised: 0 → +3 ✓\nH reduced: +1 → 0 ✓\nALLOW multiples, and ionic equation 2Al + 6H⁺ → 2Al³⁺ + 3H₂\nIGNORE state symbols\nOxidation number marks can be scored from an unbalanced equation` },
      { q: `A sample of silicon from a computer chip was analysed by mass spectrometry. The relative abundances of the ions detected are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nm/z | 28 | 29 | 30\nRelative abundance / % | 92.2 | 4.7 | 3.1\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass of silicon in this sample, to 1 decimal place, and explain why the three peaks are at different m/z values.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(28 × 92.2 + 29 × 4.7 + 30 × 3.1) / 100 ✓\nAr = 28.1 ✓ (28.109)\nPeaks are from isotopes: atoms with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons ✓\nALLOW 'different masses due to different numbers of neutrons'\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks for the calculation` },
      { q: `The boiling points of three noble gases are shown below.\n\nNoble gas | Ne | Ar | Kr\nBoiling point / °C | −246 | −186 | −153\n\nExplain the trend in boiling points.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Number of electrons increases (from Ne to Kr) ✓\nStronger London forces / induced dipole–dipole interactions between atoms ✓\nMore energy needed to overcome the London forces ✓\nALLOW 'van der Waals' forces for London forces\nDO NOT ALLOW 'breaking bonds' OR 'covalent bonds' — CON for the third mark\nIGNORE references to mass alone` },
      { q: `Ammonia, NH₃, reacts with boron trifluoride, BF₃, to form a single product, H₃NBF₃, in which nitrogen is bonded directly to boron.\n\nExplain how the N–B bond forms and predict the H–N–H bond angle in H₃NBF₃.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Dative (covalent) bond / coordinate bond ✓\nThe lone pair on N is donated to B / both shared electrons come from N (B in BF₃ has only 6 outer electrons) ✓\nN now has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so H–N–H = 109.5° ✓\nALLOW 109° to 109.5°` },
      { q: `Nitrogen has the electron configuration 1s² 2s² 2p³.\n\nDescribe the shapes of an s-orbital and a p-orbital, and describe how the three 2p electrons occupy the 2p orbitals of a nitrogen atom.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `s-orbital is spherical ✓\np-orbital is dumbbell-shaped ✓\nThe 2p electrons occupy separate 2p orbitals (one electron in each of the three orbitals) with parallel spins / before pairing ✓\nALLOW 'figure of eight' for dumbbell\nALLOW the third mark from a correct box diagram with ↑ ↑ ↑` },
      { q: `A student's method for a titration of 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide with hydrochloric acid (PAG2) is shown below.\n\n1. Rinse the burette with distilled water, then fill it with hydrochloric acid.\n2. Measure 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide into a conical flask using a 25 cm³ measuring cylinder.\n3. Add a few drops of methyl orange indicator.\n4. Add acid from the burette until the colour changes, then record the volume.\n\nSuggest three improvements to the student's method that would give more accurate results. Give a reason for each.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three of:\nRinse the burette with the hydrochloric acid (not only water), so the acid is not diluted ✓\nUse a (25.0 cm³) pipette instead of a measuring cylinder, as it has a lower (percentage) uncertainty ✓\nAdd acid dropwise near the end point / swirl the flask, so the end point is not overshot ✓\nRepeat the titration until concordant titres (within 0.10 cm³) are obtained, to identify and discount anomalies ✓\nUse a white tile under the flask, to see the colour change clearly ✓\nRead the burette at the bottom of the meniscus at eye level ✓\nEach mark needs the improvement AND the reason\nIGNORE 'use a different indicator' (methyl orange is suitable for a strong acid–strong base titration)` },
      { q: `A student titrates 25.0 cm³ of dilute sulfuric acid with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide using phenolphthalein indicator (PAG2). The mean titre is 22.60 cm³.\n\nH₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of the sulfuric acid. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(NaOH) = 0.100 × 22.60 / 1000 = 2.26 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(H₂SO₄) = 2.26 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 1.13 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\n[H₂SO₄] = 1.13 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.0452 ✓\nmol dm⁻³ answer to 3 s.f.: 0.0452 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nECF from incorrect n(NaOH) or ratio\nALLOW 1 ECF mark for 0.0904 (1 : 1 ratio used) as the final answer\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student determines the relative molecular mass of a volatile liquid alkane (PAG1). 0.168 g of the liquid is injected into a gas syringe in an oven at 100 °C. The vapour occupies 60.0 cm³ at a pressure of 101 kPa.\n\nThe liquid is one of pentane (Mr = 72.0), hexane (Mr = 86.0) or heptane (Mr = 100.0).\n\nDetermine the relative molecular mass of the liquid and identify it.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Converts units: T = 373 K, p = 101 × 10³ Pa, V = 60.0 × 10⁻⁶ m³ ✓\nn = pV / RT = (101 × 10³ × 60.0 × 10⁻⁶) / (8.314 × 373) = 1.95 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMr = 0.168 / 1.95 × 10⁻³ = 86.0 ✓\nhexane ✓ (identification must be consistent with calculated Mr)\nECF from incorrect n\nALLOW Mr in the range 85.9–86.2` },
      { q: `A student needs a 250.0 cm³ standard solution of anhydrous sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃, for a titration (PAG2). The student has calculated that 2.65 g of Na₂CO₃ is required.\n\nDescribe how the student should prepare this standard solution.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Weigh the solid accurately in a weighing bottle and reweigh the empty bottle / weigh by difference (or weigh into a beaker and rinse the weighing bottle into the beaker) ✓\nDissolve in a small volume of distilled water in a beaker, stirring ✓\nTransfer to a 250 cm³ volumetric flask, rinsing the beaker and glass rod with distilled water into the flask ✓\nMake up to the graduation mark with distilled water so the bottom of the meniscus is on the line (dropwise at the end), then stopper and invert to mix ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 'measuring cylinder' OR 'conical flask' for the volumetric flask` },
      { q: `Sodium chloride melts at 801 °C. Iodine, I₂, sublimes on gentle warming and melts at 114 °C.\n\nExplain this difference in melting points in terms of structure and bonding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `NaCl: giant ionic lattice ✓\nStrong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, requiring a lot of energy to overcome ✓\nI₂: simple molecular (lattice) ✓\nWeak London forces / induced dipole–dipole interactions between molecules need little energy to overcome ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 'covalent bonds broken' when iodine melts — CON for the fourth mark\nDO NOT ALLOW 'intermolecular forces' OR 'molecules' for NaCl` },
      { q: `Hydrogen chloride, HCl, tetrachloromethane, CCl₄, and trichloromethane, CHCl₃, all contain polar bonds. Pauling electronegativities: H 2.2, C 2.6, Cl 3.2.\n\nExplain why the H–Cl bond is polar, and explain which of CCl₄ and CHCl₃ is a polar molecule.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Electronegativity is the ability of an atom to attract the bonding electrons in a covalent bond ✓\nCl is more electronegative than H so the bonding pair is closer to Cl: Hδ+–Clδ− ✓\nCCl₄ is non-polar: tetrahedral and symmetrical, so the C–Cl dipoles cancel ✓\nCHCl₃ is polar: the C–H dipole differs from the C–Cl dipoles, so the molecule is not symmetrical and the dipoles do not cancel ✓\nALLOW 'shared pair of electrons' for 'bonding electrons'` },
      { q: `A student adds 0.120 g of magnesium ribbon to 25.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid and collects the hydrogen in a gas syringe (PAG1).\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nDetermine which reactant is in excess and calculate the maximum volume of hydrogen, in cm³, that could be collected at room temperature and pressure. Assume 1 mol of gas occupies 24.0 dm³ at RTP. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `n(Mg) = 0.120 / 24.3 = 4.94 × 10⁻³ mol AND n(HCl) = 1.00 × 25.0 / 1000 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nHCl needed = 2 × 4.94 × 10⁻³ = 9.88 × 10⁻³ mol, which is less than 0.0250 mol, so HCl is in excess ✓\nn(H₂) = n(Mg) = 4.94 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nV(H₂) = 4.94 × 10⁻³ × 24 000 = 119 cm³ ✓\nECF from incorrect n(Mg)\nALLOW 118–119 cm³` },
      { q: `Iron forms two common ions, Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺. An iron atom has 26 electrons.\n\nWrite the electron configurations of Fe, Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ using s, p and d notation, and state which electrons are removed first when an iron atom forms an ion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Fe: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s² ✓\nFe²⁺: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ ✓\nFe³⁺: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ ✓\nThe 4s electrons are removed first (before the 3d) ✓\nALLOW 4s² written before 3d⁶ for Fe\nALLOW [Ar] shorthand only if the full configuration is given for at least one species\nDO NOT ALLOW Fe²⁺ as 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `Car airbags contain sodium azide, NaN₃. In a collision it decomposes rapidly to produce nitrogen gas:\n\n2NaN₃(s) → 2Na(s) + 3N₂(g)\n\nAn airbag must inflate to 60.0 dm³ at 25 °C and 110 kPa.\n\nCalculate the minimum mass of NaN₃ needed. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Converts units: V = 0.0600 m³ (60.0 × 10⁻³ m³), p = 1.10 × 10⁵ Pa, T = 298 K ✓\nn(N₂) = pV / RT = (1.10 × 10⁵ × 0.0600) / (8.314 × 298) = 2.66 mol ✓\nn(NaN₃) = 2.66 × 2/3 = 1.78 mol ✓\nMr(NaN₃) = 23.0 + (3 × 14.0) = 65.0 AND mass = 1.776 × 65.0 = 115.4 g ✓\nanswer to 3 s.f.: 115 g ✓\nECF throughout\nALLOW 115–116 g from rounding of intermediate values\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student determines the purity of a sample of limestone by adding 2.50 g of the powdered limestone to excess hydrochloric acid in a conical flask on a balance (PAG1). A loose cotton wool plug is placed in the neck of the flask. The impurities do not react.\n\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n\nThe results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime / min | 0 | 1 | 2 | 4 | 6 | 8\nMass of flask and contents / g | 152.40 | 151.92 | 151.62 | 151.43 | 151.41 | 151.41\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of CaCO₃ in the limestone, to 3 significant figures, and explain the purpose of the cotton wool plug.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass of CO₂ = 152.40 − 151.41 = 0.99 g ✓\nn(CO₂) = n(CaCO₃) = 0.99 / 44.0 = 0.0225 mol ✓\nmass CaCO₃ = 0.0225 × 100.1 = 2.25 g ✓\n% CaCO₃ = 2.25 / 2.50 × 100 = 90.1% ✓\nCotton wool lets CO₂ escape but stops acid spray / liquid being lost from the flask (which would make the mass loss too large) ✓\nECF from incorrect mass of CO₂\nALLOW 90.0–90.1%` },
      { q: `A student is given a white solid that is a Group 1 carbonate, M₂CO₃. The student dissolves 1.38 g of the solid in distilled water and makes the solution up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask. 25.0 cm³ portions of this solution are titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid (PAG2). The mean titre is 19.95 cm³.\n\nM₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2MCl + H₂O + CO₂\n\nDetermine the identity of M. Show your working.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `n(HCl) = 0.100 × 19.95 / 1000 = 1.995 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(M₂CO₃) in 25.0 cm³ = 1.995 × 10⁻³ / 2 = 9.975 × 10⁻⁴ mol ✓\nn(M₂CO₃) in 250.0 cm³ = 9.975 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nMr(M₂CO₃) = 1.38 / 9.975 × 10⁻³ = 138 ✓\nAr(M) = (138 − 60.0) / 2 = 39(.2), so M is potassium / K ✓\nECF throughout; final mark needs an identification consistent with the student's Ar\nALLOW the final mark for comparing 138 with Mr(K₂CO₃) = 138.2` },
      { q: `A student is asked to determine the relative atomic mass of magnesium by reacting a weighed piece of magnesium ribbon with excess dilute hydrochloric acid and measuring the volume of hydrogen produced (PAG1).\n\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\n\nThe student has a 100 cm³ gas syringe, a two-decimal-place balance, 1.00 mol dm⁻³ HCl and magnesium ribbon. Assume 1 mol of gas occupies 24.0 dm³ at room temperature and pressure.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the experiment, including a suitable mass of magnesium, and explain how the results would be processed to obtain Ar(Mg). Evaluate the main sources of error and suggest how they could be reduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• Clean the ribbon with emery paper to remove the oxide layer\n• Weigh about 0.06–0.08 g of Mg (gives about 60–80 cm³ H₂, within the 100 cm³ syringe)\n• Measure an excess of HCl (e.g. 25 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³) into a conical flask with a bung and delivery tube connected to the gas syringe\n• Add the Mg (e.g. suspended or tipped in) and quickly replace the bung; wait until the syringe reading stops changing\n• Repeat with further samples / different masses\n2. Processing\n• n(H₂) = V / 24 000 (V in cm³)\n• n(Mg) = n(H₂) from 1 : 1 ratio\n• Ar = mass of Mg / n(Mg); e.g. 0.060 g gives 59 cm³, Ar = 0.060 / (59/24 000) = 24.4\n• Mean of repeats, or plot volume against mass and use the gradient\n3. Evaluation\n• Gas escapes before the bung is replaced: use a tube of acid inside the flask, or a divided flask, so reaction starts after sealing\n• Percentage uncertainty in a small mass is large (0.01 / 0.06 × 100 ≈ 17%): use a three-decimal-place balance or a longer length of ribbon with a larger syringe\n• Oxide coating gives too high an Ar; hydrogen dissolving slightly in water; syringe sticking\n• Temperature/pressure not exactly RTP: use pV = nRT with measured T and p\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `The shapes of molecules and ions can be predicted using electron-pair repulsion theory. Consider BF₃, NH₃ and H₂O.\n\nExplain the shape and bond angle of each species, and explain whether each molecule is polar. Pauling electronegativities: H 2.2, B 2.0, N 3.0, O 3.4, F 4.0.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Electron pairs and repulsion\n• BF₃: 3 bonding pairs, 0 lone pairs; NH₃: 3 bonding pairs, 1 lone pair; H₂O: 2 bonding pairs, 2 lone pairs\n• Electron pairs repel and move as far apart as possible\n• Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs\n2. Shapes and bond angles\n• BF₃ trigonal planar, 120°\n• NH₃ (trigonal) pyramidal, 107°\n• H₂O non-linear / bent, 104.5°\n• Each lone pair reduces the angle by about 2.5° from 109.5°\n3. Polarity\n• All bonds are polar because of electronegativity differences (Bδ+–Fδ−, Nδ−–Hδ+, Oδ−–Hδ+)\n• BF₃ is symmetrical, so dipoles cancel: non-polar\n• NH₃ and H₂O are not symmetrical (lone pairs), so dipoles do not cancel: polar\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `The boiling points of some hydrides of Groups 14 to 17 are shown below.\n\nGroup 14 | CH₄ −162 °C | SiH₄ −112 °C | GeH₄ −88 °C\nGroup 15 | NH₃ −33 °C | PH₃ −88 °C | AsH₃ −62 °C\nGroup 16 | H₂O 100 °C | H₂S −60 °C | H₂Se −41 °C\nGroup 17 | HF 20 °C | HCl −85 °C | HBr −67 °C\n\nAnalyse these data fully. Explain the trends shown and the anomalous values, in terms of intermolecular forces.`, marks: 6, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Trends\n• Group 14: boiling points increase CH₄ to GeH₄\n• Groups 15–17: boiling points increase from the Period 3 hydride to the Period 4 hydride\n• Increasing number of electrons, so stronger London forces / induced dipole–dipole interactions, so more energy to overcome\n2. Anomalies\n• NH₃, H₂O and HF have much higher boiling points than expected from the trend\n• N, O and F are highly electronegative and have lone pairs, so these molecules form hydrogen bonds\n• Hydrogen bonds are the strongest intermolecular force, so more energy is needed to separate the molecules\n• CH₄ shows no anomaly: C is not electronegative enough to form hydrogen bonds\n3. Comparison and extra detail\n• Groups 15–17 hydrides are polar so they also have permanent dipole–dipole interactions; Group 14 hydrides are symmetrical (tetrahedral) and non-polar, London forces only\n• H₂O has the highest boiling point: each molecule forms up to two hydrogen bonds (two lone pairs and two Hδ+), more than HF or NH₃\n• Covalent bonds are not broken on boiling\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student wants to find the concentration of ethanoic acid in a bottle of vinegar by titration with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide (PAG2). The vinegar is expected to be about 0.8 mol dm⁻³, so it must be diluted before the titration.\n\nCH₃COOH(aq) + NaOH(aq) → CH₃COONa(aq) + H₂O(l)\n\nDescribe how the student should dilute the vinegar and carry out the titration, and explain how the results would be used to calculate the concentration of ethanoic acid in the original vinegar.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Dilution\n• Pipette 25.0 cm³ of vinegar into a 250 cm³ volumetric flask (×10 dilution)\n• Make up to the mark with distilled water, bottom of meniscus on the line; stopper and invert to mix\n• Diluted acid about 0.08 mol dm⁻³ gives a titre of about 20 cm³, suitable for a 50 cm³ burette\n2. Titration\n• Rinse the pipette with diluted vinegar and the burette with NaOH(aq)\n• Pipette 25.0 cm³ of the diluted vinegar into a conical flask; add a few drops of phenolphthalein (suitable for weak acid–strong base)\n• Titrate with NaOH, swirling, adding dropwise near the end point, until the first permanent pale pink colour; white tile\n• Rough titration then repeat until concordant titres (within 0.10 cm³)\n3. Processing\n• Mean of concordant titres\n• n(NaOH) = 0.100 × mean titre / 1000 = n(CH₃COOH) in 25.0 cm³ (1 : 1)\n• [diluted acid] = n / 0.0250\n• [original vinegar] = [diluted acid] × 10\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'M3': {
    name: 'Periodic Table and Energy',
    questions: [
      { q: `The melting points of the Period 2 elements lithium to carbon are shown below.\nLi 454 K   Be 1560 K   B 2350 K   C 3823 K (diamond)\n\nIdentify the type of structure that boron and carbon both have.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Giant covalent (lattice) ✓\nALLOW macromolecular / giant molecular / network covalent\nDO NOT ALLOW giant metallic OR simple molecular` },
      { q: `Magnesium reacts only very slowly with cold water but reacts rapidly when heated in steam, glowing with a bright white light.\n\nWrite the equation for the reaction of magnesium with steam.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW Mg(OH)₂ as product (this is the product with cold water)` },
      { q: `Farmers sometimes spread calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂, on fields.\n\nState why calcium hydroxide is added to fields.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `To neutralise acidic soil ✓\nALLOW to raise the pH of the soil / to reduce soil acidity\nIGNORE fertiliser` },
      { q: `A student adds aqueous silver nitrate to a solution suspected of containing a halide ion. Before adding the silver nitrate, the student first adds dilute nitric acid.\n\nExplain why dilute nitric acid is added first.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `To remove / react with any carbonate ions (or other ions) which would otherwise form a precipitate with Ag⁺ ✓\nALLOW to prevent a false positive from Ag₂CO₃\nDO NOT ALLOW to remove sulfate ions\nIGNORE to acidify the solution (without reason)` },
      { q: `A student warms a white solid with aqueous sodium hydroxide. A gas is given off which turns damp red litmus paper blue.\n\nIdentify the gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Ammonia / NH₃ ✓\nIGNORE ammonium` },
      { q: `A student dissolves 5.0 g of ammonium nitrate in 50 cm³ of water in a polystyrene cup. The temperature of the solution falls from 20.5 °C to 13.0 °C.\n\nDeduce the sign of the enthalpy change of solution of ammonium nitrate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Positive / ΔH is + (the process is endothermic) ✓\nALLOW endothermic, so ΔH is positive` },
      { q: `The standard enthalpy change of formation of methane, ΔfH⦵, is −75 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nState the standard conditions of temperature and pressure used for this value.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `298 K AND 100 kPa ✓\nALLOW 25 °C for 298 K\nALLOW 1 bar OR 101 kPa for 100 kPa\nBoth needed for the mark` },
      { q: `In the Haber process, nitrogen and hydrogen gases react over a solid iron catalyst.\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nState the type of catalyst used.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Heterogeneous ✓\n(catalyst is in a different phase / state from the reactants)` },
      { q: `Sulfur dioxide reacts with oxygen in the Contact process using a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst.\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\n\nState the effect, if any, of the catalyst on the position of equilibrium.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `No effect / position of equilibrium does not change ✓\nALLOW the catalyst increases the rate of forward and reverse reactions equally\nIGNORE equilibrium is reached faster` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide, a brown gas, forms an equilibrium mixture with colourless dinitrogen tetroxide.\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)\n\nWrite an expression for Kc for this equilibrium.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Kc = [N₂O₄(g)] / [NO₂(g)]² ✓\nSquare brackets required\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW expression inverted OR [2NO₂]` },
      { q: `The atomic radii of some Period 3 elements are given below.\nNa 0.186 nm   Mg 0.160 nm   Al 0.143 nm   Si 0.118 nm   P 0.110 nm\n\nExplain why atomic radius decreases across Period 3.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Nuclear charge increases / number of protons increases ✓\nElectrons are added to the same shell, so shielding is similar AND outer electrons are attracted more strongly / pulled closer to the nucleus ✓\nALLOW 'same energy level' for same shell\nDO NOT ALLOW 'no shielding' OR 'shielding decreases'\nIGNORE effective nuclear charge` },
      { q: `The boiling points of the halogens are shown below.\nCl₂ 239 K   Br₂ 332 K   I₂ 457 K\n\nExplain the trend in boiling point down the group.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Number of electrons increases down the group ✓\nStronger London forces (induced dipole–dipole interactions) between molecules, so more energy is needed to overcome them ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking covalent bonds / bonds within molecules (scores 0 for the second mark)\nALLOW van der Waals' forces for London forces` },
      { q: `Chlorine is added to drinking water. It reacts with water as shown.\nCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ HCl(aq) + HClO(aq)\n\nUse oxidation numbers to explain why this is a disproportionation reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Oxidation number of Cl changes from 0 to −1 in HCl AND from 0 to +1 in HClO ✓\nChlorine is both oxidised and reduced (in the same reaction) ✓\nALLOW the same element is simultaneously oxidised and reduced\nSecond mark can be awarded only if oxidation numbers are correct or ECF from a single error` },
      { q: `Chlorine is used to treat drinking water in the UK. Some people argue that the use of chlorine should be reduced.\n\nState one benefit and one risk of using chlorine in water treatment.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Benefit: kills bacteria / micro-organisms / pathogens, preventing diseases such as cholera or typhoid ✓\nRisk: chlorine is toxic (gas) OR chlorine reacts with organic matter to form chlorinated hydrocarbons which may cause cancer ✓\nALLOW 'sterilises water' for benefit\nIGNORE 'kills germs' alone / 'harmful' alone` },
      { q: `Barium reacts vigorously with dilute hydrochloric acid, giving off a gas and forming a colourless solution.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction and state the change in oxidation number of barium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ba + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂ ✓\nOxidation number of Ba changes from 0 to +2 ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW ionic equation Ba + 2H⁺ → Ba²⁺ + H₂\nALLOW 'increases by 2' for the second mark` },
      { q: `A student adds a small amount of calcium oxide to water and shakes the mixture. Some of the solid dissolves. The student then tests the solution with universal indicator.\n\nWrite the equation for the reaction of calcium oxide with water and suggest the pH of the solution formed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ ✓\npH in the range 11–13 ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW any single value from 11 to 13\nDO NOT ALLOW pH 14 OR 'alkaline' with no value` },
      { q: `A student has a solution that contains both carbonate ions and sulfate ions. The student adds aqueous barium chloride directly to the solution, without first adding acid, and a white precipitate forms.\n\nExplain why the student cannot conclude from this result that sulfate ions are present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Carbonate ions also give a white precipitate with Ba²⁺ / barium carbonate, BaCO₃, is also a white precipitate ✓\nThe solution should first be acidified with dilute acid (e.g. dilute HNO₃) to remove carbonate ions (as CO₂) ✓\nALLOW the precipitate could be BaCO₃ instead of / as well as BaSO₄` },
      { q: `A student adds dilute nitric acid to a few drops of aqueous sodium carbonate. Effervescence is seen.\n\nWrite the ionic equation for the reaction and describe how the student could confirm the identity of the gas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O ✓\nBubble the gas through limewater, which turns milky / cloudy ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW calcium hydroxide solution for limewater\nDO NOT ALLOW full equation with Na₂CO₃ and HNO₃ for the first mark` },
      { q: `PAG3. A student measures the temperature change when excess zinc powder is added to aqueous copper(II) sulfate. The thermometer has an uncertainty of ±0.5 °C in each reading. The temperature change recorded is 25.5 °C.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the temperature change.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Two readings, so uncertainty = 2 × 0.5 = ±1.0 °C ✓\n% uncertainty = (1.0 / 25.5) × 100 = 3.92% ✓\nALLOW 1.96% for 1 mark (only one reading counted)\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Hydrogen and iodine react in a sealed flask to form an equilibrium mixture at 763 K.\nH₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)\nAt equilibrium: [H₂] = 0.00450 mol dm⁻³, [I₂] = 0.00450 mol dm⁻³, [HI] = 0.0305 mol dm⁻³\n\nCalculate the value of Kc at 763 K.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Kc = [HI]² / ([H₂][I₂]) = 0.0305² / (0.00450 × 0.00450) ✓\nKc = 45.9 (no units) ✓\nALLOW 45.94 / 45.938…\nIGNORE 'no units' statement\nDO NOT ALLOW units for the second mark\nCorrect answer with no working scores both marks` },
      { q: `Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is used as a fuel. Its standard enthalpy change of formation is −277 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nDefine the term standard enthalpy change of formation, and write the equation, with state symbols, that represents ΔfH⦵ of ethanol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `The enthalpy change when 1 mole of a compound is formed from its elements (in their standard states) under standard conditions ✓\n2C(s) + 3H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → C₂H₅OH(l) ✓\nState symbols required for the second mark\nALLOW 298 K and 100 kPa for standard conditions\nDO NOT ALLOW 'energy needed'` },
      { q: `A student reacts magnesium ribbon with 1.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid and then repeats the experiment using 2.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid. All other conditions are kept the same.\n\nExplain, in terms of collision theory, why the rate is greater with 2.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `More (acid) particles in the same volume ✓\nMore frequent collisions / more collisions per unit time (so more successful collisions per second) ✓\nIGNORE 'more collisions' without reference to time / frequency for the second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW particles have more energy` },
      { q: `Dinitrogen tetroxide, a colourless gas, dissociates into brown nitrogen dioxide.\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)   ΔH = +57 kJ mol⁻¹\nA sealed syringe containing the equilibrium mixture is placed in hot water.\n\nPredict the colour change seen and explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Mixture becomes darker brown ✓\n(Forward reaction is endothermic, so) position of equilibrium shifts to the right to oppose the increase in temperature / to absorb the heat ✓\nALLOW shifts towards NO₂\nSecond mark depends on correct direction of shift` },
      { q: `Many industrial processes, such as the manufacture of ammonia and sulfuric acid, use catalysts.\n\nExplain why catalysts are important for both economic and environmental reasons.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Economic: reactions can be carried out at lower temperatures, reducing energy costs / increasing the rate so more product is made in a given time ✓\nEnvironmental: less fuel is burned / less energy is used, so less CO₂ is released OR processes with higher atom economy / less waste can be used ✓\nALLOW fewer fossil fuels burned\nIGNORE 'cheaper' alone` },
      { q: `PAG3. A student mixes 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid with 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ aqueous sodium hydroxide in a polystyrene cup. The maximum temperature rise is 13.4 °C.\nAssume the density of the mixture is 1.00 g cm⁻³ and its specific heat capacity is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of neutralisation, ΔneutH, in kJ mol⁻¹.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = mcΔT = 50.0 × 4.18 × 13.4 = 2800.6 J ✓\nn(H₂O formed) = n(HCl) = 2.00 × 25.0/1000 = 0.0500 mol ✓\nΔneutH = −2.8006 / 0.0500 = −56.0 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nNegative sign required for the third mark\nECF from incorrect q or n\nALLOW −56.01\nDO NOT ALLOW m = 25.0 g (gives −28.0; award ECF for third mark only)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Propane is burned as a fuel.\nC₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(g)\nAverage bond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–C 347, C–H 413, O=O 498, C=O 805, O–H 464\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change for this reaction using the average bond enthalpies.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Bonds broken = (2 × 347) + (8 × 413) + (5 × 498) = 6488 kJ ✓\nBonds formed = (6 × 805) + (8 × 464) = 8542 kJ ✓\nΔH = 6488 − 8542 = −2054 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nECF from incorrect bonds broken or bonds formed\nALLOW +2054 for 2 marks (sign reversed)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Iron is extracted from haematite in the blast furnace.\nFe₂O₃(s) + 3CO(g) → 2Fe(s) + 3CO₂(g)\nStandard enthalpy changes of formation / kJ mol⁻¹: Fe₂O₃(s) −824, CO(g) −111, CO₂(g) −394\n\nCalculate the standard enthalpy change for this reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΣΔfH(products) = 3 × (−394) = −1182 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΣΔfH(reactants) = −824 + 3 × (−111) = −1157 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔrH = −1182 − (−1157) = −25 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW ΔfH(Fe) = 0 stated or used\nECF from one incorrect sum\nALLOW +25 for 2 marks (sign reversed)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of formation of ethanol cannot be measured directly. It can be calculated from enthalpy changes of combustion.\nΔcH⦵ / kJ mol⁻¹: C(s) −394, H₂(g) −286, C₂H₅OH(l) −1367\n2C(s) + 3H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → C₂H₅OH(l)\n\nCalculate the standard enthalpy change of formation of ethanol.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΣΔcH(reactants) = 2 × (−394) + 3 × (−286) = −1646 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔfH = ΣΔcH(reactants) − ΣΔcH(products) = −1646 − (−1367) ✓\nΔfH = −279 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nECF from incorrect sum\nALLOW +279 for 2 marks (sign reversed)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide and oxygen form an equilibrium mixture with sulfur trioxide.\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\nAn equilibrium mixture at a certain temperature contains (data written for this practice question):\n[SO₂] = 0.0400 mol dm⁻³, [O₂] = 0.0200 mol dm⁻³, [SO₃] = 0.360 mol dm⁻³\n\nCalculate the value of Kc and give its units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Kc = [SO₃]² / ([SO₂]² [O₂]) ✓\nKc = 0.360² / (0.0400² × 0.0200) = 4050 ✓\nUnits: dm³ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 4.05 × 10³\nECF for value from an incorrect expression\nUnits mark is independent: ALLOW mol⁻¹ dm³` },
      { q: `A student adds 0.200 g of calcium to excess water. The calcium reacts to form a cloudy alkaline solution and a gas.\nAssume the gas is collected at room temperature and pressure, where 1 mol of gas occupies 24.0 dm³.\n\nCalculate the volume of gas produced, in cm³.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂ (1 : 1 ratio of Ca : H₂ used) ✓\nn(Ca) = 0.200 / 40.1 = 4.99 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nV(H₂) = 4.99 × 10⁻³ × 24 000 = 120 cm³ ✓\nALLOW 119.7 cm³\nALLOW 0.120 dm³ only if units changed correctly\nECF from incorrect n(Ca)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `Magnesium hydroxide is used as an antacid to treat indigestion caused by excess stomach acid. One antacid tablet contains 0.250 g of Mg(OH)₂.\nMg(OH)₂(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + 2H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the volume, in cm³, of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid that one tablet can neutralise.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(Mg(OH)₂) = 0.250 / 58.3 = 4.29 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(HCl) = 2 × 4.29 × 10⁻³ = 8.58 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nV = 8.58 × 10⁻³ / 0.100 × 1000 = 85.8 cm³ ✓\nECF from incorrect n(Mg(OH)₂) or ratio\nALLOW 42.9 cm³ for 2 marks (1 : 1 ratio used)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A gaseous reaction mixture is heated from 300 K to 350 K. The activation energy, Ea, of the reaction stays the same.\n\nSketch the Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies at 300 K and at 350 K on the same axes. Label the axes, label each curve and mark Ea.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `Axes labelled: y-axis number of molecules AND x-axis energy ✓\n300 K curve starting at the origin, with a single peak and not touching the x-axis at high energy ✓\n350 K curve with peak lower AND shifted to the right of the 300 K peak, and above the 300 K curve at higher energies (beyond Ea) ✓\nALLOW fraction / proportion of molecules for y-axis\nDO NOT ALLOW curves that meet or cross the x-axis at high energy\nIGNORE relative areas if approximately similar` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes slowly at room temperature. The decomposition is exothermic. The reaction is much faster when solid manganese(IV) oxide is added.\n2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\n\nDraw an enthalpy profile diagram for the uncatalysed and catalysed decomposition. Label ΔH and the activation energy of the catalysed reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Products drawn lower than reactants, with axes / y-axis labelled enthalpy ✓\nΔH shown as arrow from reactant level down to product level (downwards arrow or labelled negative) ✓\nTwo humps: catalysed curve lower than uncatalysed AND Ea(cat) labelled from reactant level to the top of the lower hump ✓\nALLOW reactants labelled 2H₂O₂ and products 2H₂O + O₂\nDO NOT ALLOW Ea measured from product level` },
      { q: `PAG4. A student has a solution of an unknown sodium halide. The student adds dilute nitric acid followed by aqueous silver nitrate and a cream precipitate forms.\n\nDescribe how the student could use aqueous ammonia to confirm the identity of the halide. Include the expected observations and an ionic equation for the formation of the precipitate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add dilute NH₃(aq): precipitate does not dissolve ✓\nAdd concentrated NH₃(aq): precipitate dissolves, so the halide is bromide ✓\nAg⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s) ✓\nState symbols required in the equation\nALLOW 'partially soluble in dilute ammonia' for first mark\nDO NOT ALLOW AgNO₃ in the equation (not ionic)` },
      { q: `The melting points of sodium and magnesium are given below.\nNa 371 K   Mg 923 K\n\nExplain why magnesium has a much higher melting point than sodium.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Both have giant metallic lattices / metallic bonding ✓\nMg²⁺ has a greater charge (and smaller radius) than Na⁺ AND magnesium has more delocalised electrons (2 per atom compared with 1) ✓\nStronger attraction between the positive ions and delocalised electrons, so more energy is needed to overcome the metallic bonding ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW reference to intermolecular forces / covalent bonds / ionic bonding (scores 0 for the third mark)\nALLOW 'sea of electrons' for delocalised electrons` },
      { q: `PAG3. A student determines the enthalpy change of combustion of methanol using a spirit burner to heat 100 cm³ of water in a copper can. The student's value is −480 kJ mol⁻¹. The data book value is −726 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nSuggest three reasons why the student's value is less exothermic than the data book value. For each reason, suggest one improvement to the method that would reduce the error.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three reason + matching improvement pairs, 1 mark each ✓✓✓\n• Heat lost to the surroundings (air) → insulate the can / use a draught shield / put a lid on the can ✓\n• Incomplete combustion (soot formed) → ensure a good supply of oxygen / adjust the flame / use a fully enclosed combustion calorimeter with oxygen ✓\n• Heat absorbed by the copper can / apparatus → include the heat capacity of the can in the calculation ✓\n• Evaporation of methanol from the wick → put the cap on the burner immediately after the experiment and reweigh quickly ✓\n• Data book value is under standard conditions (water formed as liquid) → (no improvement needed, but ALLOW as a reason if explained) ✓\nEach mark needs reason AND linked improvement\nIGNORE 'human error' / 'repeat the experiment'` },
      { q: `PAG3. A student burns propan-1-ol, C₃H₇OH, in a spirit burner to heat 150 g of water. The results are shown below.\nMass of spirit burner before burning / g: 54.312\nMass of spirit burner after burning / g: 53.712\nTemperature of water at start / °C: 19.5\nTemperature of water at end / °C: 48.0\nThe specific heat capacity of water is 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of combustion of propan-1-ol in kJ mol⁻¹, to 3 significant figures, and suggest why the value is less exothermic than the data book value of −2021 kJ mol⁻¹.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 150 × 4.18 × 28.5 = 17 869.5 J ✓\nn(C₃H₇OH) = 0.600 / 60.0 = 0.0100 mol ✓\nΔcH = −17.8695 / 0.0100 = −1790 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nHeat lost to the surroundings OR incomplete combustion OR heat absorbed by the apparatus ✓\nNegative sign AND 3 s.f. required for the third mark\nALLOW −1787 for 2 of the 3 calculation marks (not 3 s.f.)\nECF from incorrect q or n\nIGNORE 'human error'` },
      { q: `The first ionisation energies of the Period 3 elements are shown below.\nNa 496   Mg 738   Al 578   Si 789   P 1012   S 1000   Cl 1251   Ar 1521 (all in kJ mol⁻¹)\n\nExplain why the first ionisation energy of aluminium is lower than that of magnesium, and why the first ionisation energy of sulfur is lower than that of phosphorus.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Al: outer electron is in a 3p (sub-shell) whereas Mg outer electron is in 3s ✓\n3p electron is at a higher energy / further from the nucleus / has some extra shielding from the 3s electrons, so is removed more easily ✓\nS: the electron is removed from a 3p orbital that contains two (paired) electrons ✓\nRepulsion between the paired electrons means less energy is needed to remove one of them ✓\nALLOW 'orbital' for sub-shell where meaning is clear\nDO NOT ALLOW 'shell' for sub-shell in the Al explanation` },
      { q: `PAG9. A student adds excess marble chips to 50.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid and collects the carbon dioxide in a gas syringe. The results are shown below (data written for this practice question).\nTime / s: 0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120, 150, 180\nVolume of CO₂ / cm³: 0, 28, 48, 62, 71, 77, 80, 82, 82\n\nCalculate the mean rate of reaction, in cm³ s⁻¹, between 0 s and 40 s and between 100 s and 150 s. Explain, in terms of collisions, why the rate changes during the reaction.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0–40 s: 48 / 40 = 1.2 cm³ s⁻¹ ✓\n100–150 s: (82 − 77) / 50 = 0.10 cm³ s⁻¹ ✓\nConcentration of HCl decreases as it is used up ✓\nSo collisions between acid particles and the marble surface become less frequent ✓\nALLOW 1.20 and 0.1\nIGNORE fewer collisions without reference to frequency / time for the fourth mark\nDO NOT ALLOW marble chips are used up (they are in excess)` },
      { q: `PAG4. A student investigates the reactivity of the halogens. Aqueous chlorine, bromine and iodine are each added to separate aqueous solutions of potassium chloride, potassium bromide and potassium iodide. Cyclohexane is then added to each tube and the tube shaken.\n\nDescribe what the student would observe in the cyclohexane layer when aqueous chlorine is added to potassium bromide and to potassium iodide. Write an ionic equation for one of these reactions and explain what the results show about the trend in reactivity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Cl₂ + KBr: cyclohexane layer turns orange ✓\nCl₂ + KI: cyclohexane layer turns violet / purple ✓\nCl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ OR Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂ ✓\nReactivity (oxidising power) decreases down the group / chlorine is the strongest oxidising agent so displaces bromide and iodide ✓\nALLOW orange-red / yellow-orange for bromine\nDO NOT ALLOW brown for the cyclohexane layer with iodine\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Household bleach is made by reacting chlorine with cold, dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction. Use oxidation numbers to explain why the reaction is a disproportionation, and state the name of the active ingredient in bleach.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O ✓\nCl: 0 → −1 in NaCl ✓\nCl: 0 → +1 in NaClO, so chlorine is both oxidised and reduced ✓\nSodium chlorate(I) ✓\nALLOW ionic equation Cl₂ + 2OH⁻ → Cl⁻ + ClO⁻ + H₂O\nALLOW NaOCl\nALLOW sodium hypochlorite\nDO NOT ALLOW sodium chlorate / sodium chlorate(V)` },
      { q: `A student adds small, equal masses of calcium and barium to separate beakers of water. Barium reacts much more vigorously than calcium.\n\nWrite the equation, with state symbols, for the reaction of barium with water, and explain why barium is more reactive than calcium.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Ba(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Ba(OH)₂(aq) + H₂(g) ✓\nBarium atoms are larger / outer electrons are further from the nucleus AND there is more shielding (more inner shells) ✓\nLess attraction between the nucleus and the outer electrons ✓\nSo the (first and second) ionisation energies are lower / the outer electrons are lost more easily, so Ba is a stronger reducing agent ✓\nState symbols required for the first mark\nIGNORE 'nuclear charge increases' if outweighed by shielding and distance\nDO NOT ALLOW 'barium gains electrons more easily'` },
      { q: `When concentrated hydrochloric acid is added to a pink solution containing [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions, the following equilibrium is set up.\n[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq) + 4Cl⁻(aq) ⇌ [CoCl₄]²⁻(aq) + 6H₂O(l)\npink                                      blue\nWhen the equilibrium mixture is placed in a beaker of hot water, the solution turns blue. When water is added to the equilibrium mixture, it turns pink.\n\nExplain these observations and deduce the sign of the enthalpy change of the forward reaction.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Heating: position of equilibrium shifts to the right / towards [CoCl₄]²⁻ ✓\nSystem opposes the increase in temperature, so the forward reaction is endothermic / ΔH is positive ✓\nAdding water: the position of equilibrium shifts to the left / towards [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ✓\nTo oppose the increase in concentration of water / Cl⁻ is diluted ✓\nALLOW system minimises the change for 'opposes'\nSecond mark requires a statement of sign or endothermic` },
      { q: `PAG3. The enthalpy change for the thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate cannot be measured directly.\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\nA student carries out two experiments, each using 50.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid (an excess).\nExperiment 1: 2.50 g of CaCO₃ added; temperature rise 1.9 °C. CaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)\nExperiment 2: 1.40 g of CaO added; temperature rise 23.0 °C. CaO(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l)\nAssume the solution has density 1.00 g cm⁻³ and specific heat capacity 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change for the thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate, in kJ mol⁻¹, to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Expt 1: q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 1.9 = 397 J AND n(CaCO₃) = 2.50 / 100.1 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nΔH₁ = −0.397 / 0.0250 = −15.9 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nExpt 2: q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 23.0 = 4807 J AND n(CaO) = 1.40 / 56.1 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nΔH₂ = −4.807 / 0.0250 = −193 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nΔdecompH = ΔH₁ − ΔH₂ = −15.9 − (−193) = +177 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW +176 to +177 depending on rounding\nPositive sign required for the final mark\nECF from incorrect ΔH₁ or ΔH₂\nALLOW −177 for 4 marks (wrong way round in the Hess cycle)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `An antacid tablet contains calcium carbonate as its only basic ingredient. A student adds one tablet, of mass 1.25 g, to 50.0 cm³ of 0.500 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid (an excess). When the reaction has finished, the excess acid is titrated with 0.500 mol dm⁻³ aqueous sodium hydroxide. The mean titre is 22.80 cm³.\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of calcium carbonate in the tablet.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(HCl) at start = 0.500 × 50.0/1000 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nn(HCl) in excess = n(NaOH) = 0.500 × 22.80/1000 = 0.0114 mol ✓\nn(HCl) reacted with CaCO₃ = 0.0250 − 0.0114 = 0.0136 mol ✓\nn(CaCO₃) = 0.0136 / 2 = 0.00680 mol; mass = 0.00680 × 100.1 = 0.681 g ✓\n% CaCO₃ = 0.681 / 1.25 × 100 = 54.5% ✓\nALLOW 54.4% to 54.5% depending on rounding\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `PAG3. A student determines the enthalpy change for the reaction below by adding excess zinc powder to 50.0 cm³ of 0.500 mol dm⁻³ aqueous copper(II) sulfate in a polystyrene cup. The temperature is recorded every 30 s before and after adding the zinc. The student plots a temperature–time graph and extrapolates the cooling curve back to the time of mixing to obtain a corrected temperature rise of 25.5 °C.\nZn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s)\nAssume the solution has density 1.00 g cm⁻³ and specific heat capacity 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of this reaction in kJ mol⁻¹ to 3 significant figures. Explain why the student extrapolates the cooling curve and why the zinc is used in excess.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 25.5 = 5329.5 J ✓\nn(CuSO₄) = 0.500 × 50.0/1000 = 0.0250 mol ✓\nΔrH = −5.3295 / 0.0250 = −213 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nExtrapolation: to compensate for heat lost to the surroundings while the reaction takes place / gives the temperature rise if the reaction were instantaneous ✓\nExcess zinc: ensures all the CuSO₄ reacts, so the moles reacting are known from the CuSO₄ ✓\nNegative sign required for the third mark\nECF from incorrect q or n\nALLOW −213.2` },
      { q: `The melting points of the Period 3 elements are shown below.\nNa 371 K   Mg 923 K   Al 933 K   Si 1687 K   P (white) 317 K   S 388 K   Cl 172 K   Ar 84 K\n\nExplain the trend in melting points across Period 3 in terms of the structure and bonding of the elements.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Na to Al (giant metallic)\n• Giant metallic lattice with metallic bonding between positive ions and delocalised electrons\n• From Na to Al the ionic charge increases (+1 to +3), ionic radius decreases and the number of delocalised electrons increases\n• Stronger metallic bonding, so more energy is needed to overcome it, so melting point increases\n2. Si (giant covalent)\n• Giant covalent lattice\n• Many strong covalent bonds must be broken, so silicon has the highest melting point\n3. P to Ar (simple molecular)\n• Simple molecular: P₄, S₈, Cl₂ molecules and Ar atoms\n• Weak London forces between molecules / atoms are overcome, so melting points are low\n• Order S₈ ≥ P₄ ≥ Cl₂ ≥ Ar follows the number of electrons: more electrons gives stronger London forces\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `PAG4. A technician has four unlabelled bottles, A, B, C and D. Each contains one of the following aqueous solutions: ammonium chloride, sodium carbonate, potassium sulfate and sodium bromide.\nThe technician has the following reagents: dilute nitric acid, aqueous barium nitrate, aqueous silver nitrate, aqueous sodium hydroxide, aqueous ammonia, limewater and red litmus paper.\n\nPlan a sequence of tests that would identify the solution in each bottle. Explain the order of tests and include the expected observations and ionic equations.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Order of tests\n• Carbonate test first, then sulfate, then halide\n• Nitric acid removes CO₃²⁻ which would otherwise give a white precipitate with Ba²⁺ (BaCO₃) or with Ag⁺ (Ag₂CO₃)\n• Barium nitrate used rather than barium chloride, so that no Cl⁻ is added before the halide test\n• Sulfate test before halide test because Ag₂SO₄ may also precipitate\n2. Tests and observations\n• Add dilute HNO₃: effervescence; gas turns limewater milky → sodium carbonate\n• Add Ba(NO₃)₂(aq): white precipitate → potassium sulfate\n• Add HNO₃ then AgNO₃(aq): white precipitate soluble in dilute NH₃ → chloride; cream precipitate soluble only in concentrated NH₃ → bromide\n• Warm with NaOH(aq): gas turns damp red litmus blue → ammonium chloride\n3. Equations\n• CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O\n• Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)\n• Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) AND Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq) → AgBr(s)\n• NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Methanol is manufactured from carbon monoxide and hydrogen.\nCO(g) + 2H₂(g) ⇌ CH₃OH(g)   ΔH = −91 kJ mol⁻¹\nA typical plant operates at about 250 °C and 50–100 atm using a copper-based catalyst.\n\nExplain why these conditions are chosen. In your answer, use le Chatelier's principle and collision theory, and discuss the economic and safety factors involved.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Temperature\n• Forward reaction is exothermic, so a low temperature shifts the position of equilibrium to the right, increasing the yield of methanol\n• At low temperature the rate is too slow: fewer molecules have energy ≥ Ea\n• 250 °C is a compromise between a reasonable yield and a reasonable rate\n2. Pressure\n• 3 moles of gas on the left and 1 mole on the right, so high pressure shifts the position of equilibrium to the right, increasing yield\n• High pressure also increases rate: more particles per unit volume so more frequent collisions\n• Very high pressures are expensive (energy for compressors, strong pipes / plant) and a safety risk, so 50–100 atm is a compromise\n3. Catalyst and economics\n• The catalyst provides an alternative route with lower activation energy, so more molecules have energy ≥ Ea at a lower temperature\n• Catalyst does not affect the position of equilibrium but allows equilibrium to be reached faster\n• Lower temperature reduces energy costs and CO₂ emissions; unreacted gases are recycled\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `PAG9. Magnesium ribbon reacts with hydrochloric acid.\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\nA student is provided with magnesium ribbon, 2.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid, distilled water and standard laboratory apparatus.\n\nPlan an experiment, using continuous monitoring, to investigate how the concentration of hydrochloric acid affects the initial rate of this reaction. Describe how the results would be processed to reach a conclusion.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• Dilute the 2.00 mol dm⁻³ HCl using a burette / volumetric pipette and distilled water to make a range of concentrations (e.g. 0.40, 0.80, 1.20, 1.60, 2.00 mol dm⁻³) with the same total volume\n• Use the same length / mass of magnesium ribbon (cleaned), with acid in large excess\n• Collect H₂ in a gas syringe connected to a conical flask with a bung; start the timer as the Mg is added\n• Record the volume of gas at regular intervals (e.g. every 10 s) until the reaction stops\n2. Control and safety\n• Keep the temperature constant (e.g. water bath) and use the same volume of acid each time\n• H₂ is flammable: no naked flames; HCl is an irritant: wear eye protection\n3. Processing and conclusion\n• Plot a graph of volume of H₂ against time for each concentration\n• Draw a tangent at t = 0 and determine its gradient to give the initial rate (cm³ s⁻¹)\n• Plot initial rate against concentration; conclusion e.g. initial rate increases as concentration increases (directly proportional if a straight line through the origin)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'M4': {
    name: 'Core Organic Chemistry',
    questions: [
      { q: `A chlorinated solvent impurity found in a sample of recycled oil has the structural formula (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CHClCH₃.\n\nState the systematic name of this compound.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `2-chloro-4-methylpentane ✓\nALLOW 2-chloro-4-methyl pentane / missing hyphens\nDO NOT ALLOW 4-chloro-2-methylpentane (locants must follow alphabetical order when tied)` },
      { q: `Hexane, C₆H₁₄, is one of the alkanes present in petrol.\n\nWrite the empirical formula of hexane.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₃H₇ ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW C₆H₁₄ / H₇C₃ written as a ratio only` },
      { q: `Butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH, can be made by fermentation and is being trialled as a biofuel for cars.\n\nWrite the equation for the complete combustion of butan-1-ol.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₄H₉OH + 6O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O ✓\nALLOW any correct structural or molecular formula for butan-1-ol, e.g. C₄H₁₀O\nALLOW multiples\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `A student adds aqueous silver nitrate dissolved in ethanol to 1-iodobutane and warms the mixture in a water bath at 50 °C. A yellow precipitate forms. The water in the solution acts as the nucleophile and hydrolyses the haloalkane.\n\nWrite the equation for the hydrolysis of 1-iodobutane by water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂I + H₂O → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + H⁺ + I⁻ ✓\nALLOW C₄H₉I + H₂O → C₄H₉OH + HI\nALLOW molecular formulae\nIGNORE the subsequent Ag⁺ + I⁻ → AgI step if also given` },
      { q: `Dichlorodifluoromethane, CF₂Cl₂, was used as a refrigerant. In the stratosphere, ultraviolet radiation breaks one bond in each molecule. Average bond enthalpies: C–F +467 kJ mol⁻¹, C–Cl +346 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nExplain why chlorine radicals rather than fluorine radicals are formed from CF₂Cl₂ in the stratosphere.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The C–Cl bond is weaker / has a lower bond enthalpy than C–F, so C–Cl is broken by UV radiation (and C–F is not) ✓\nALLOW C–F bond too strong to be broken by UV\nDO NOT ALLOW C–Cl bond is more polar` },
      { q: `In a preparation of cyclohexene, a student places cyclohexanol and concentrated phosphoric acid in a pear-shaped flask and adds a few anti-bumping granules before heating.\n\nState why the anti-bumping granules are added.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `To ensure smooth boiling / to prevent large bubbles forming (violent boiling) ✓\nALLOW to stop the mixture boiling over / jumping\nIGNORE to stop the mixture bumping (repeats question) unless explained` },
      { q: `But-1-ene reacts with hydrogen bromide at room temperature to form 2-bromobutane as the major product.\n\nState the type of reaction and mechanism taking place.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Electrophilic addition ✓\nBOTH words needed\nDO NOT ALLOW nucleophilic addition / addition alone` },
      { q: `The mass spectrum of propan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH, has a molecular ion peak at m/z = 60 and a large fragment ion peak at m/z = 31.\n\nIdentify the species responsible for the peak at m/z = 31.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `CH₂OH⁺ ✓\nPositive charge required\nALLOW [CH₃O]⁺ / CH₃O⁺\nDO NOT ALLOW CH₂OH (no charge) / CH₂OH•` },
      { q: `Ethene, H₂C=CH₂, is the starting material for the manufacture of poly(ethene). Each carbon atom in ethene forms three σ-bonds and one π-bond.\n\nState the H–C–H bond angle in ethene.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `120° ✓\nALLOW 117–122°` },
      { q: `Margarine is made by partially hydrogenating unsaturated vegetable oils. In the laboratory the same type of reaction converts hex-1-ene into hexane using hydrogen gas at about 150 °C.\n\nState the catalyst used in this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Nickel / Ni ✓\nALLOW platinum / palladium\nIGNORE finely divided` },
      { q: `Two reactions involving the breaking of a carbon–halogen bond are shown.\nReaction 1: CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl• + Cl• (ultraviolet radiation in the stratosphere)\nReaction 2: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + Br⁻ (aqueous NaOH)\n\nExplain the difference between the type of bond fission taking place in Reaction 1 and in Reaction 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Reaction 1 is homolytic fission: each bonding atom receives one electron from the bonding pair, forming two radicals ✓\nReaction 2 is heterolytic fission: one bonding atom (Br) receives both electrons from the bonding pair, forming ions ✓\nALLOW shared pair / covalent bond for bonding pair\nDO NOT ALLOW 'each atom gets one electron' without reference to the bonding pair for the first mark only if no idea of the bond splitting is given` },
      { q: `Alkenes such as propene, CH₃CH=CH₂, are much more reactive than alkanes, and compounds such as but-2-ene exist as two stereoisomers.\n\nDescribe how the π-bond in an alkene is formed and explain why it prevents rotation about the C=C bond.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Sideways overlap of adjacent p-orbitals (one on each carbon) above and below the plane of the carbon atoms ✓\nRotation would break the overlap / break the π-bond, so the π-bond locks the C=C in position ✓\nALLOW diagram showing p-orbital overlap\nDO NOT ALLOW overlap of s-orbitals / end-on overlap for the first mark` },
      { q: `In a poorly maintained car engine, some of the octane in petrol undergoes incomplete combustion, forming carbon monoxide and water only.\n\nWrite the equation for this incomplete combustion of octane, C₈H₁₈, and explain why carbon monoxide is toxic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₈H₁₈ + 8½O₂ → 8CO + 9H₂O ✓\nCO binds to haemoglobin (in red blood cells), reducing the transport of oxygen in the blood ✓\nALLOW multiples, e.g. 2C₈H₁₈ + 17O₂ → 16CO + 18H₂O\nALLOW CO forms carboxyhaemoglobin\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `PAG7 — Qualitative analysis of organic functional groups.\nA student is given two unlabelled bottles. One contains cyclohexane and the other contains cyclohexene. Both are colourless liquids.\n\nDescribe a chemical test the student could use to identify which bottle contains cyclohexene. Include the observations for both liquids.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add bromine water and shake ✓\nCyclohexene: orange (or yellow/brown) to colourless; cyclohexane: stays orange / no change ✓\nALLOW Br₂ in an organic solvent\nDO NOT ALLOW 'goes clear' for colourless\nThe observation mark requires the correct reagent` },
      { q: `Perspex is the addition polymer formed from the monomer methyl 2-methylpropenoate, CH₂=C(CH₃)COOCH₃.\n\nDraw the repeat unit of Perspex.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Backbone of two carbon atoms with only single bonds, –CH₂–C(CH₃)(COOCH₃)– ✓\nOpen (continuation) bonds at both ends of the backbone, through/extending beyond the brackets ✓\nALLOW COOCH₃ / CO₂CH₃ shown as a condensed side group\nALLOW n outside brackets; IGNORE missing n\nDO NOT ALLOW a C=C double bond in the backbone (0 marks)` },
      { q: `Some carrier bags are now made from poly(lactic acid), a polymer made from plant starch, while others are made from poly(ethene) containing additives that make the polymer photodegradable.\n\nOutline why these polymers are an improvement on conventional poly(ethene) in terms of waste disposal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Biodegradable polymers (e.g. poly(lactic acid)) are broken down by microorganisms (into CO₂ and water), so do not persist in landfill ✓\nPhotodegradable polymers contain bonds that are weakened by absorbing light, so the polymer breaks down when exposed to light ✓\nALLOW poly(lactic acid) made from a renewable resource / not from crude oil as an alternative first mark\nIGNORE 'environmentally friendly' unqualified` },
      { q: `A student converts butan-1-ol into 1-bromobutane by heating it under reflux with sodium bromide and concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nWrite the overall equation for this reaction and state the role of the hydrogen bromide formed in the mixture.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + NaBr + H₂SO₄ → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br + NaHSO₄ + H₂O ✓\nHBr (formed from NaBr and H₂SO₄) reacts with the alcohol / provides the bromide for substitution of the –OH group ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + HBr → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br + H₂O for the first mark\nALLOW 2NaBr + H₂SO₄ forming Na₂SO₄ if balanced\nALLOW molecular formulae C₄H₉OH and C₄H₉Br` },
      { q: `Nitrogen monoxide radicals, NO•, released by high-flying aircraft can also catalyse the breakdown of ozone in the stratosphere. The overall reaction is O₃ + O → 2O₂.\n\nWrite two equations to show how NO• radicals catalyse this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `NO• + O₃ → NO₂• + O₂ ✓\nNO₂• + O → NO• + O₂ ✓\nALLOW radical dots anywhere on the radical; IGNORE missing dots\nALLOW the two equations in either order` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nA student sets up Quickfit apparatus to distil propanal from a reaction mixture. The student's description of the set-up is:\n• "the thermometer bulb dips into the liquid in the flask"\n• "cooling water enters the condenser at the top and leaves at the bottom".\n\nFor each error, state the correct arrangement.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `Thermometer bulb should be level with / just below the side-arm (opening to the condenser), to measure the temperature of the vapour being distilled ✓\nWater should enter the condenser at the bottom and leave at the top (so that the condenser is filled with water / more efficient cooling) ✓\nALLOW thermometer at the neck of the still head\nIGNORE reasons unless contradictory` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nAfter separating crude 1-bromobutane from the aqueous layer, a student adds a small amount of anhydrous calcium chloride to the cloudy organic liquid in a conical flask and swirls it.\n\nExplain the purpose of the anhydrous calcium chloride and state how the student would know when enough has been added.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Drying agent: removes (traces of) water from the organic liquid ✓\nThe liquid turns from cloudy to clear / the solid no longer clumps together but moves freely when swirled ✓\nALLOW anhydrous MgSO₄ or Na₂SO₄ named in place of CaCl₂ in the explanation\nDO NOT ALLOW 'dehydrates the 1-bromobutane'` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nTo oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid, a student heats the alcohol with excess acidified potassium dichromate(VI) for 30 minutes under reflux, using a pear-shaped flask with a vertical Liebig condenser.\n\nExplain why heating under reflux is used, and state why the top of the condenser must not be sealed with a stopper.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Reflux allows prolonged heating without loss of volatile reactants/products: vapours condense and return to the flask (so the reaction goes to completion / ethanal intermediate is fully oxidised) ✓\nA sealed system would build up pressure and could explode / shatter the glassware ✓\nALLOW 'closed system' for sealed\nIGNORE references to the reaction being slow unless linked to prolonged heating` },
      { q: `Carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour are greenhouse gases. Each molecule contains bonds that absorb infrared radiation at characteristic wavenumbers, e.g. C=O in CO₂ absorbs at 2350 cm⁻¹.\n\nExplain how the absorption of infrared radiation by these gases is linked to global warming.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The bonds in the molecules absorb infrared radiation (emitted from the Earth's surface), making the bonds vibrate more ✓\nThe energy is re-emitted, some back towards the Earth, so the lower atmosphere/Earth warms up ✓\nALLOW 'trap heat' only for the second mark if linked to re-emission or the atmosphere warming\nDO NOT ALLOW absorption of UV radiation` },
      { q: `Roadside evidential breathalysers measure the concentration of ethanol in a driver's breath using infrared spectroscopy. Breath also contains water vapour.\n\nExplain how infrared spectroscopy is used to measure the ethanol concentration, and why the O–H absorption is not used.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The absorbance/intensity of a characteristic ethanol absorption, e.g. C–H at about 2850–3000 cm⁻¹ (or C–O at about 1050 cm⁻¹), is measured; the greater the absorbance, the greater the ethanol concentration ✓\nWater vapour in breath also has an O–H absorption, which would interfere / give a falsely high reading ✓\nALLOW C–H absorption within 2850–3100 cm⁻¹\nALLOW 'amount of IR absorbed is proportional to concentration'` },
      { q: `The mass spectrum of butane, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃, has a molecular ion peak at m/z = 58 and a large fragment ion peak at m/z = 43.\n\nWrite an equation to show the formation of the fragment ion at m/z = 43 from the molecular ion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `[CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃]⁺ → [CH₃CH₂CH₂]⁺ + CH₃• ✓✓\nOne mark for the correct ions (molecular ion C₄H₁₀⁺ and fragment C₃H₇⁺, both with + charges)\nOne mark for the uncharged CH₃• radical as the other product\nALLOW C₄H₁₀⁺ → C₃H₇⁺ + CH₃\nDO NOT ALLOW CH₃⁺ / CH₃⁻ as the other product` },
      { q: `C₅H₁₂ has three structural isomers. Their boiling points are shown (data written for this practice question, based on literature values).\npentane, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃: 36 °C\n2-methylbutane, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₃: 28 °C\n2,2-dimethylpropane, C(CH₃)₄: 10 °C\n\nExplain the trend in boiling points.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `More branching → fewer points of contact / less surface contact between molecules ✓\nWeaker London (induced dipole–dipole) forces between molecules ✓\nLess energy needed to overcome the London forces, so boiling point decreases from pentane to 2,2-dimethylpropane ✓\nALLOW van der Waals' forces / dispersion forces for London forces\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking covalent bonds (third mark not available)\nThe isomers have the same number of electrons, so the difference is due to shape — ALLOW as part of mark 1` },
      { q: `The molecular formula C₄H₉Br has four structural isomers that are bromoalkanes. Each is a possible impurity in a sample of 1-bromobutane.\n\nDraw the skeletal formulae of all four isomers, and identify the isomer that is a tertiary haloalkane.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Correct skeletal formulae for 1-bromobutane and 2-bromobutane ✓\nCorrect skeletal formulae for 1-bromo-2-methylpropane and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane ✓\nTertiary: 2-bromo-2-methylpropane / (CH₃)₃CBr ✓\nALLOW a name or a correct structure for the tertiary isomer\nDO NOT ALLOW structures showing C or H atoms in the chain for 'skeletal' (penalise once only)` },
      { q: `A student reacts propane with chlorine in the presence of ultraviolet radiation, hoping to make 1-chloropropane. Analysis of the product mixture shows 1-chloropropane, 2-chloropropane and several dichloropropanes.\n\nExplain why radical substitution gives this mixture of products.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Substitution can occur at different positions along the carbon chain / Cl• can remove an H atom from C-1 or C-2 ✓\nso both CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl and CH₃CHClCH₃ are formed ✓\nFurther substitution: Cl• can remove H from a chloropropane molecule, forming C₃H₆Cl• which reacts with Cl₂ (to give C₃H₆Cl₂) ✓\nALLOW a propagation equation such as C₃H₇Cl + Cl• → C₃H₆Cl• + HCl for the third mark\nIGNORE termination steps` },
      { q: `Pent-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃, is formed when 2-bromopentane is heated with ethanolic potassium hydroxide. Pent-2-ene exists as two stereoisomers.\n\nDraw and label the E and Z isomers of pent-2-ene, and explain why pent-2-ene shows E/Z isomerism.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Z isomer drawn with CH₃ and C₂H₅ on the same side of C=C, labelled Z ✓\nE isomer drawn with CH₃ and C₂H₅ on opposite sides, labelled E ✓\nRestricted rotation about the C=C (π-bond) AND each carbon of C=C is attached to two different groups ✓\nALLOW cis for Z and trans for E if also labelled E/Z correctly\nC=C bond angles must look approximately 120° — IGNORE slight distortions\nOne mark only if both structures correct but E/Z labels swapped` },
      { q: `The compound ClCH=C(Br)CH₃ (2-bromo-1-chloroprop-1-ene) exists as two stereoisomers. In isomer P, the Cl atom and the Br atom are on the same side of the C=C double bond.\n\nDeduce whether isomer P is the E or the Z isomer, and explain why the cis–trans naming system cannot be used for this compound.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `On C-1, Cl has higher priority than H (higher atomic number); on C-2, Br has higher priority than CH₃ (Br atomic number 35 greater than C 6) ✓\nHigher-priority groups on the same side, so P is the Z isomer ✓\nCis–trans needs one of the groups on each carbon of the C=C to be the same; here the four groups (H, Cl, Br, CH₃) are all different ✓\nZ with no priority reasoning scores mark 2 only\nALLOW CIP / Cahn–Ingold–Prelog rules` },
      { q: `Propan-2-ol, used in hand gels, is manufactured by the direct hydration of propene. A small amount of an isomeric alcohol is also formed.\n\nState the reagent and catalyst used for this reaction, and name the major and minor alcohol products.`, marks: 3, command: 'State', markScheme: `Steam / H₂O(g) AND phosphoric acid / H₃PO₄ catalyst ✓\nMajor product: propan-2-ol ✓\nMinor product: propan-1-ol ✓\nALLOW H⁺ / acid catalyst for phosphoric acid\nIGNORE temperature and pressure (e.g. 300 °C, 60–70 atm)\nDO NOT ALLOW water (l) / aqueous` },
      { q: `A local council collects waste plastic which contains poly(ethene) and poly(chloroethene), PVC. Landfill space is limited.\n\nOutline three ways in which waste polymers can be processed, other than landfill, giving one problem specific to PVC.`, marks: 3, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Recycling: polymers are sorted by type, then melted/remoulded into new products ✓\nCombustion for energy production (heat used to generate electricity) ✓\nUse as an organic feedstock, e.g. cracked to make monomers/chemicals/plastics AND PVC releases HCl (toxic/corrosive) when burned, which must be removed (e.g. by neutralising with a base in the waste gases) ✓\nThe third mark requires the feedstock use and the PVC problem\nALLOW PVC problem attached to either combustion or recycling (e.g. HCl removed and solvents used to dissolve PVC)\nIGNORE biodegradable polymers` },
      { q: `Ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH (Mr = 46.0), boils at 78 °C and mixes completely with water. Propane, CH₃CH₂CH₃ (Mr = 44.0), boils at −42 °C and is almost insoluble in water.\n\nExplain these differences in properties.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Ethanol molecules form hydrogen bonds (between the O–H group of one molecule and a lone pair on O of another); propane has only London forces ✓\nHydrogen bonds are stronger than London forces, so more energy is needed to separate ethanol molecules (higher boiling point) ✓\nEthanol forms hydrogen bonds with water molecules, so it dissolves; propane cannot ✓\nALLOW intermolecular forces in ethanol are stronger due to hydrogen bonding\nDO NOT ALLOW breaking of covalent bonds / O–H bonds when boiling (max 2)\nThe similar Mr means London forces are similar — credit as part of mark 1` },
      { q: `PAG7 — Qualitative analysis of organic functional groups.\nA student labels three alcohols A, B and C. They are known to be butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol, in no particular order. The student warms 1 cm³ of each with acidified potassium dichromate(VI) in a water bath (data written for this practice question).\nA: solution turns from orange to green\nB: solution stays orange\nC: solution turns from orange to green\n\nIdentify B, explain your answer, and state further information needed to distinguish A from C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B is 2-methylpropan-2-ol ✓\nIt is a tertiary alcohol: no H atom on the carbon bonded to the –OH group, so it is not oxidised (and Cr₂O₇²⁻ is not reduced to Cr³⁺) ✓\nA and C are the primary and secondary alcohol; the oxidation products must be identified, e.g. an aldehyde/carboxylic acid (from butan-1-ol) versus a ketone (from butan-2-ol), e.g. by IR — broad O–H 2500–3300 cm⁻¹ for the acid after reflux ✓\nALLOW tests on the distillate showing an aldehyde for butan-1-ol\nIGNORE named reagents not in Module 4 if the idea of identifying the product type is clear` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nA student prepares cyclohexene, C₆H₁₀, by dehydrating 10.0 cm³ of cyclohexanol, C₆H₁₁OH (density 0.962 g cm⁻³), with concentrated phosphoric acid. After purification, 4.92 g of cyclohexene is obtained.\nC₆H₁₁OH → C₆H₁₀ + H₂O\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of cyclohexene. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mass of cyclohexanol = 10.0 × 0.962 = 9.62 g AND n(cyclohexanol) = 9.62/100.0 = 0.0962 mol ✓\nTheoretical mass of cyclohexene = 0.0962 × 82.0 = 7.89 g ✓\nPercentage yield = 4.92/7.89 × 100 = 62.4% ✓\nALLOW n(cyclohexene) = 4.92/82.0 = 0.0600 mol, then 0.0600/0.0962 × 100 = 62.4%\nECF from incorrect n(cyclohexanol)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nALLOW 62.3% from use of rounded intermediate values` },
      { q: `Compound D is a colourless liquid with molecular formula C₃H₆O₂. Its infrared spectrum shows the following absorptions (data written for this practice question):\n• a very broad absorption from 2500 to 3300 cm⁻¹\n• a strong, sharp absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹\n• an absorption at 1240 cm⁻¹\n\nUse the OCR Data Sheet ranges to identify the bonds responsible and deduce the structure of D.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `2500–3300 cm⁻¹ (broad): O–H in a carboxylic acid ✓\n1715 cm⁻¹: C=O (and 1240 cm⁻¹: C–O) ✓\nD is propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH ✓\nALLOW 1630–1820 cm⁻¹ quoted for C=O; 1000–1300 cm⁻¹ for C–O\nDO NOT ALLOW O–H in an alcohol for the first mark\nALLOW correct structural/displayed/skeletal formula for D` },
      { q: `Butane and 2-methylpropane are structural isomers, C₄H₁₀. Both mass spectra show a molecular ion peak at m/z = 58 and peaks at m/z = 43 and m/z = 15. Only one of the spectra has a significant peak at m/z = 29.\n\nIdentify the ion at m/z = 29 and explain which isomer gives this peak.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `m/z = 29 is C₂H₅⁺ / CH₃CH₂⁺ ✓\nButane gives the peak at m/z = 29 ✓\nFission of the central C–C bond in CH₃CH₂–CH₂CH₃ gives C₂H₅⁺; 2-methylpropane, (CH₃)₃CH, has no C₂H₅ group / breaking any single C–C bond only gives CH₃ and C₃H₇ fragments ✓\nCharge required on the ion for mark 1` },
      { q: `Cyclohexane reacts with bromine in the presence of ultraviolet radiation to form bromocyclohexane.\nC₆H₁₂ + Br₂ → C₆H₁₁Br + HBr\n\nWrite equations for the initiation, propagation and termination steps of this radical substitution mechanism.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Initiation: Br₂ → 2Br• ✓\nPropagation 1: C₆H₁₂ + Br• → C₆H₁₁• + HBr ✓\nPropagation 2: C₆H₁₁• + Br₂ → C₆H₁₁Br + Br• ✓\nTermination: any one of C₆H₁₁• + Br• → C₆H₁₁Br OR 2C₆H₁₁• → C₁₂H₂₂ OR 2Br• → Br₂ ✓\nALLOW radical dot anywhere on the radical; penalise missing dots once only\nALLOW the two propagation steps in either order\nALLOW correct skeletal formulae` },
      { q: `But-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃, reacts rapidly with bromine at room temperature to form 2,3-dibromobutane.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Include curly arrows, relevant dipoles and the structure of the intermediate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Dipole on Br–Br shown as Brδ+–Brδ− (induced by the π-electrons of the C=C) ✓\nCurly arrow from the C=C (π-bond) to the Brδ+ AND curly arrow from the Br–Br bond to the Brδ− ✓\nCorrect carbocation CH₃CH⁺–CHBrCH₃ AND Br⁻ with a lone pair ✓\nCurly arrow from a lone pair on Br⁻ to C⁺ AND product CH₃CHBrCHBrCH₃ ✓\nCurly arrows must start at a bond or a lone pair\nALLOW full negative charge on Br⁻ shown with one lone pair only\nDO NOT ALLOW a δ+ / δ− dipole on C=C` },
      { q: `A student oxidises propan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂OH (boiling point 97 °C), using acidified potassium dichromate(VI). The product depends on the apparatus used. Propanal boils at 48 °C and propanoic acid at 141 °C.\n\nExplain how the student can obtain propanal in one experiment and propanoic acid in another. Include an equation for each oxidation, using [O] for the oxidising agent.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Propanal: heat and distil off the product as it forms (with the alcohol in excess / dichromate added dropwise) ✓\nso propanal (lowest boiling point) leaves the mixture before it is further oxidised ✓\nPropanoic acid: heat under reflux with excess acidified dichromate (so any aldehyde is returned and fully oxidised) ✓\nCH₃CH₂CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CH₂CHO + H₂O AND CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + 2[O] → CH₃CH₂COOH + H₂O ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH₂CHO + [O] → CH₃CH₂COOH for the second equation\nALLOW C₂H₅CHO / C₂H₅COOH\nIGNORE colour change` },
      { q: `1-Bromopropane is heated under reflux with aqueous sodium hydroxide to form propan-1-ol.\nCH₃CH₂CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + Br⁻\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Show curly arrows, relevant dipoles and lone pairs, and name the mechanism.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Dipole on C–Br: Cδ+–Brδ− ✓\nCurly arrow from a lone pair on the O of OH⁻ to the δ+ carbon ✓\nCurly arrow from the C–Br bond to Br, with products CH₃CH₂CH₂OH and Br⁻ ✓\nNucleophilic substitution ✓\nThe curly arrow from OH⁻ must start at a lone pair or the negative charge on O\nALLOW SN2 transition state drawn correctly if the arrows are shown\nDO NOT ALLOW curly arrow from H of OH⁻` },
      { q: `PAG7 — Qualitative analysis of organic functional groups.\nA student investigates the rate of hydrolysis of three haloalkanes. 1 cm³ of ethanol containing silver nitrate is added to 5 drops of each haloalkane in a test tube, in a water bath at 50 °C. The time for a precipitate to appear is recorded (data written for this practice question).\n1-chlorobutane: white precipitate after 22 min\n1-bromobutane: cream precipitate after 4 min\n1-iodobutane: yellow precipitate after 45 s\nBond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–Cl 346, C–Br 290, C–I 228\n\nExplain the order of reactivity, and state why ethanol is used as a solvent and why a water bath is used.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Rate of hydrolysis: 1-iodobutane fastest, then 1-bromobutane, then 1-chlorobutane slowest ✓\nC–I has the lowest bond enthalpy / is the weakest bond, so it is broken most easily; bond enthalpy (not polarity) determines the rate ✓\nEthanol dissolves both the haloalkane and the aqueous silver nitrate / haloalkanes are insoluble in water ✓\nWater bath keeps all three tubes at the same (controlled) temperature, so the comparison is fair ✓\nALLOW the C–Cl bond is the most polar but reacts slowest, showing bond strength is the deciding factor\nDO NOT ALLOW 'C–I bond is most polar' as the explanation` },
      { q: `Chlorofluorocarbons such as trichlorofluoromethane, CCl₃F, drift into the stratosphere, where ultraviolet radiation produces chlorine radicals. Chlorine radicals catalyse the decomposition of ozone.\n\nWrite equations to show the formation of chlorine radicals from CCl₃F and the two propagation steps in the breakdown of ozone. Write the overall equation for the breakdown of ozone.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CCl₃F → CCl₂F• + Cl• ✓\nCl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂ ✓\nClO• + O → Cl• + O₂ ✓\nOverall: O₃ + O → 2O₂ ✓\nALLOW radical dot anywhere on the radical; penalise missing dots once only\nALLOW the two propagation steps in either order\nIGNORE 'UV' written above the arrow` },
      { q: `Butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃, is used as an industrial solvent. A chemist plans to make butanone from but-1-ene in two stages.\n\nOutline the two-stage synthesis. For each stage, state the reagents and conditions, and give the structure of the intermediate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Stage 1: steam AND phosphoric acid / H₃PO₄ catalyst ✓\nIntermediate: butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (major product) ✓\nStage 2: acidified potassium dichromate(VI) / K₂Cr₂O₇ and H₂SO₄ ✓\nHeat under reflux ✓\nALLOW Cr₂O₇²⁻/H⁺\nALLOW 'heat' for the final mark only if reflux or distillation is not contradicted\nDO NOT ALLOW butan-1-ol as the intermediate (max 3)\nIGNORE temperature and pressure for stage 1` },
      { q: `2-Methylpropene, (CH₃)₂C=CH₂, reacts with hydrogen bromide to give two possible products, 2-bromo-2-methylpropane and 1-bromo-2-methylpropane. One product is formed in much larger amounts.\n\nDraw the mechanism for the formation of the major product and explain why it is the major product.`, marks: 5, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Dipole Hδ+–Brδ− AND curly arrow from the C=C to the H of HBr ✓\nCurly arrow from the H–Br bond to Br ✓\nTertiary carbocation (CH₃)₃C⁺ AND Br⁻ with a lone pair; curly arrow from the lone pair to C⁺, giving (CH₃)₃CBr ✓\nMajor product forms via the tertiary carbocation, which is more stable than the primary carbocation (CH₃)₂CHCH₂⁺ ✓\nMore alkyl groups attached to C⁺ donate / push electron density towards the positive charge, stabilising it (Markownikoff's rule) ✓\nCurly arrows must start at a bond or a lone pair\nALLOW 'inductive effect' for electron donation\nDO NOT ALLOW the alkyl groups are electron-withdrawing` },
      { q: `Butanoic acid, CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH, is responsible for the smell of rancid butter. A student plans to make butanoic acid from 1-bromobutane in two stages.\n\nDescribe the two-stage synthesis. For each stage, state the reagents and conditions, and write an equation for each stage, using [O] for the oxidising agent where needed.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Stage 1: aqueous NaOH (or KOH), heat under reflux ✓\nCH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br + NaOH → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + NaBr ✓\nStage 2: (excess) acidified potassium dichromate(VI), K₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄ ✓\nHeat under reflux ✓\nCH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH + 2[O] → CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH + H₂O ✓\nALLOW ionic equation with OH⁻ for stage 1\nDO NOT ALLOW ethanolic NaOH (elimination) for the first mark\nALLOW C₄H₉OH and C₃H₇COOH\nECF for the stage 2 equation from an incorrect alcohol only if a primary alcohol` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nA student prepares 2-chloro-2-methylpropane (boiling point 51 °C, density 0.84 g cm⁻³) using this method.\n1. Shake 10 cm³ of 2-methylpropan-2-ol with 30 cm³ of concentrated hydrochloric acid in a separating funnel for 20 minutes.\n2. Run off and discard the aqueous layer.\n3. Add sodium hydrogencarbonate solution to the organic layer and shake, inverting the funnel and opening the tap frequently.\n4. Run off the aqueous layer, then transfer the organic layer to a conical flask and add anhydrous sodium sulfate.\n5. Redistil the liquid.\n\nExplain the purpose of steps 2 to 5, including which layer is discarded in step 2 and the temperature range collected in step 5.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Step 2: the aqueous layer is the lower layer, because 2-chloro-2-methylpropane is less dense than water (0.84 g cm⁻³) ✓\nStep 3: NaHCO₃ neutralises / removes remaining HCl (acid) from the organic layer ✓\nOpening the tap releases pressure from the CO₂ gas produced ✓\nStep 4: anhydrous Na₂SO₄ is a drying agent which removes water ✓\nStep 5: redistillation removes remaining organic impurities (e.g. unreacted alcohol); collect the fraction distilling at about 49–53 °C ✓\nALLOW any range that includes 51 °C and spans no more than about 5 °C\nALLOW 'CO₂ forms' with the idea of pressure build-up` },
      { q: `PAG5 — Synthesis of an organic liquid.\nCyclohexene (boiling point 83 °C, density 0.81 g cm⁻³) can be made in the laboratory by heating cyclohexanol (boiling point 161 °C) with concentrated phosphoric acid.\nC₆H₁₁OH → C₆H₁₀ + H₂O\n\nDescribe how a student could prepare a pure, dry sample of cyclohexene from cyclohexanol, and how the student could show that the product is cyclohexene free from cyclohexanol. Include details of apparatus and the reasons for each stage.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Preparation\n• Mix cyclohexanol with concentrated phosphoric acid (acid catalyst / dehydrating agent) in a pear-shaped / round-bottomed flask\n• Add anti-bumping granules for smooth boiling\n• Set up Quickfit distillation apparatus: still head with thermometer at the side-arm, Liebig condenser with water in at the bottom, receiver cooled in ice\n• Heat gently (e.g. with an electric heating mantle) and distil; cyclohexene (83 °C) distils as it forms; keep temperature below about 90 °C so that little cyclohexanol (161 °C) distils over\n2. Purification\n• Transfer the distillate to a separating funnel; shake with water / saturated NaCl(aq) (and Na₂CO₃(aq) to remove acid), releasing pressure\n• Cyclohexene is the upper layer (less dense than water); run off and discard the lower aqueous layer\n• Dry the organic layer with anhydrous CaCl₂ or MgSO₄ until the liquid is clear; decant or filter off the drying agent\n• Redistil, collecting the fraction boiling at about 81–85 °C\n3. Confirming the product\n• Boiling point / distillation temperature close to 83 °C indicates purity\n• Bromine water is decolourised (orange to colourless) — C=C present\n• IR spectrum: no broad O–H absorption at 3200–3600 cm⁻¹, so no cyclohexanol; C=C absorption around 1620–1680 cm⁻¹\n• Mass spectrum: molecular ion peak at m/z = 82\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student is given an unknown alcohol, A. The student carries out tests and obtains spectra (data written for this practice question).\n• Mass spectrum of A: molecular ion peak at m/z = 74; other peaks at m/z = 59, 45 and 29.\n• IR spectrum of A: broad absorption at 3350 cm⁻¹; absorption at 1110 cm⁻¹; no absorption between 1630 and 1820 cm⁻¹.\n• A is heated under reflux with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). The solution turns from orange to green and organic compound B is formed. The IR spectrum of B shows a strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹ and no broad absorption between 2500 and 3600 cm⁻¹.\n• A is heated with concentrated phosphoric acid. A mixture of three alkenes, C, D and E, is formed, all with molecular formula C₄H₈.\n\nIdentify A to E. Explain your reasoning using all of the evidence.`, marks: 6, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Evidence from spectra of A\n• M⁺ at m/z = 74 → Mr = 74, so molecular formula C₄H₁₀O (C₄H₉OH)\n• Broad absorption at 3350 cm⁻¹ (3200–3600 cm⁻¹): O–H in an alcohol; 1110 cm⁻¹: C–O; no C=O\n• m/z = 59: [C₂H₅CH(OH)]⁺ from loss of CH₃; m/z = 45: [CH₃CH(OH)]⁺ from loss of C₂H₅; m/z = 29: C₂H₅⁺\n2. Evidence from oxidation\n• Orange to green: A is oxidised (Cr₂O₇²⁻ reduced to Cr³⁺), so A is primary or secondary, not tertiary\n• B has C=O (1715 cm⁻¹) but no O–H (no broad absorption), so B is a ketone, not a carboxylic acid or alcohol\n• So A is a secondary alcohol: A is butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃; B is butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃\n• CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₂CH₃ + H₂O\n3. Evidence from dehydration\n• Dehydration (elimination of water) can remove H from C-1 or C-3\n• C: but-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃\n• D and E: E-but-2-ene and Z-but-2-ene (stereoisomers of CH₃CH=CHCH₃; restricted rotation about C=C, two different groups on each C)\n• The three alkenes confirm the –OH is on C-2 of an unbranched chain\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `PAG7 — Qualitative analysis of organic functional groups.\nA technician has five unlabelled bottles, each containing one of these colourless liquids:\ncyclohexane, cyclohexene, propan-1-ol, 2-methylpropan-2-ol, 1-bromobutane.\n\nDescribe a sequence of chemical tests that would allow a student to identify all five liquids. Give the reagents, conditions and expected observations, and explain how each result identifies the functional group present.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Test for the alkene\n• Add bromine water to a sample of each liquid and shake\n• Only cyclohexene decolourises bromine water (orange to colourless): electrophilic addition across C=C\n• Doing this test first removes the alkene before the oxidation test\n2. Tests for the alcohols\n• Warm fresh samples of the remaining four with acidified potassium dichromate(VI), K₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄, in a water bath\n• Propan-1-ol: orange to green — primary alcohol oxidised, Cr₂O₇²⁻ reduced to Cr³⁺\n• 2-methylpropan-2-ol: stays orange — tertiary alcohol, no H on the C bonded to OH, not oxidised\n• 2-methylpropan-2-ol can be distinguished from cyclohexane because it mixes with water whereas cyclohexane forms a separate layer\n3. Test for the haloalkane and conclusion\n• Add ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate to the remaining samples and warm at about 50 °C\n• 1-bromobutane: cream precipitate (AgBr) — hydrolysis releases Br⁻, Ag⁺ + Br⁻ → AgBr\n• Cyclohexane gives no change in any test (alkane, unreactive with polar reagents)\n• Safety: use a fume cupboard / no naked flames as liquids are flammable; dichromate is toxic — wear gloves\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A company needs to make 2-bromopropane, CH₃CHBrCH₃. Two routes are suggested.\nRoute 1: propane + bromine in the presence of ultraviolet radiation.\nRoute 2: propene + hydrogen bromide at room temperature.\n\nEvaluate the two routes. Include the mechanism of each reaction (as equations or a description of curly arrows), and explain which route is better for producing 2-bromopropane.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Route 1 — radical substitution\n• Initiation: Br₂ → 2Br• (homolytic fission by UV)\n• Propagation: C₃H₈ + Br• → C₃H₇• + HBr; C₃H₇• + Br₂ → C₃H₇Br + Br•\n• Termination, e.g. C₃H₇• + Br• → C₃H₇Br; 2C₃H₇• → C₆H₁₄\n• Substitution at different positions gives a mixture of 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane\n• Further substitution gives dibromopropanes etc.; HBr is a by-product, so lower atom economy\n2. Route 2 — electrophilic addition\n• HBr is polar (Hδ+–Brδ−); curly arrow from C=C to Hδ+, curly arrow from H–Br bond to Br\n• Carbocation intermediate; Br⁻ lone pair attacks C⁺\n• Secondary carbocation CH₃C⁺HCH₃ is more stable than primary CH₃CH₂CH₂⁺ (more electron-donating alkyl groups), so 2-bromopropane is the major product (Markownikoff)\n• Only one product (addition) — 100% atom economy\n3. Conclusion\n• Route 2 is better: higher yield of 2-bromopropane, little separation needed, no by-product, no UV needed\n• Route 1 needs separation of the product mixture (e.g. fractional distillation) and gives a low yield of the desired isomer\n• Route 1 starting material (propane) is cheaper/more readily available from crude oil — a possible advantage\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  },
  'M5': {
    name: 'Physical Chemistry and Transition Elements',
    questions: [
      { q: `Nitrogen monoxide reacts with oxygen:\n2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g)\nA chemist measured the initial rate of this reaction at constant temperature (data written for this practice question).\n\nExperiment 1: [NO] = 0.0100 mol dm⁻³, [O₂] = 0.0100 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 2.50 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\nExperiment 2: [NO] = 0.0200 mol dm⁻³, [O₂] = 0.0100 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 1.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\nExperiment 3: [NO] = 0.0200 mol dm⁻³, [O₂] = 0.0300 mol dm⁻³, initial rate = 3.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n\nDeduce the order of reaction with respect to NO and with respect to O₂. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `NO: 2nd order AND from Expts 1 → 2, [NO] doubles ([O₂] constant) and rate increases × 4 (= 2²) ✓\nO₂: 1st order AND from Expts 2 → 3, [O₂] triples ([NO] constant) and rate increases × 3 ✓\nIGNORE references to the stoichiometric coefficients\nDO NOT ALLOW orders with no supporting comparison of experiments` },
      { q: `Nitrogen monoxide reacts with oxygen:\n2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g)\nThe rate equation is: rate = k[NO]²[O₂]\nIn one experiment at 298 K, [NO] = 0.0100 mol dm⁻³, [O₂] = 0.0100 mol dm⁻³ and the initial rate was 2.50 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the value of the rate constant, k, at 298 K. Give your answer to 3 significant figures and state its units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `k = rate / ([NO]²[O₂]) = 2.50 × 10⁻⁵ / (0.0100² × 0.0100) ✓\nk = 25.0 ✓\nUnits: dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹ (any order)\nCorrect numerical answer with no working scores first 2 marks\nUnits mark is independent of numerical value` },
      { q: `Nitric acid, HNO₃, is a strong monobasic acid.\n\nCalculate the pH of 0.0350 mol dm⁻³ HNO₃(aq). Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pH = −log(0.0350) = 1.46 ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.5 (must be 2 d.p.)` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes in the presence of a catalyst:\n2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\nA student followed the concentration of H₂O₂ over time at constant temperature (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime / s: 0, 650, 1300, 1950\n[H₂O₂] / mol dm⁻³: 0.800, 0.400, 0.200, 0.100\n\nShow that the reaction is first order with respect to H₂O₂ and calculate the rate constant, k. Give your answer to 3 significant figures, with units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `Half-life is constant: [H₂O₂] halves every 650 s ✓\n(constant half-life shows) first order ✓\nk = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693 / 650 = 1.07 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 1.066 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ to 1.07 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹\nUnits s⁻¹ required for the third mark` },
      { q: `Cyclopropane isomerises to propene at 750 K. The reaction is first order with respect to cyclopropane and the half-life is 6.5 minutes.\n\nState what is meant by the term half-life of a reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The time taken for the concentration (or amount) of a reactant to fall to half of its initial value ✓\nALLOW time for half of the reactant to be used up\nDO NOT ALLOW half the time for the reaction to finish` },
      { q: `A student investigated three separate reactions. Reaction X is zero order, reaction Y is first order and reaction Z is second order with respect to reactant A.\n\nSketch rate–concentration graphs (rate on the vertical axis, [A] on the horizontal axis) for each of the three reactions. Label each graph.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `X (zero order): horizontal straight line – rate independent of [A] ✓\nY (first order): straight line through the origin ✓\nZ (second order): upward curve through the origin with increasing gradient ✓\nALLOW three separate sketches or three lines on one set of axes if labelled\nDO NOT ALLOW a second-order curve that does not start at the origin` },
      { q: `A student used an iodine clock reaction to investigate how [I⁻] affects the rate of reaction between peroxodisulfate ions and iodide ions:\nS₂O₈²⁻(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → 2SO₄²⁻(aq) + I₂(aq)\nEach mixture contained the same volumes of S₂O₈²⁻(aq), Na₂S₂O₃(aq) and starch. Different volumes of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ KI(aq) were used, with water added so the total volume was always 50.0 cm³. The time, t, for the blue-black colour to appear was recorded (data written for this practice question).\n\nVolume of KI(aq) / cm³: 10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0\nt / s: 84, 42, 28, 21\n\nAnalyse the results to determine the order of reaction with respect to I⁻. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `[I⁻] is proportional to volume of KI(aq) because total volume is constant ✓\n1/t values: 0.0119, 0.0238, 0.0357, 0.0476 s⁻¹ OR doubling [I⁻] halves t (84 → 42 s) ✓\n1/t (∝ initial rate) is directly proportional to [I⁻], so first order with respect to I⁻ ✓\nALLOW rate ∝ 1/t used as relative rate\nALLOW tripling volume (10 → 30 cm³) reduces t to one third (84 → 28 s)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'first order' with no supporting use of the data` },
      { q: `In an iodine clock experiment, a small fixed amount of sodium thiosulfate is added to each reaction mixture. The thiosulfate reacts with the iodine as it forms. When all the thiosulfate is used up, free iodine reacts with starch and the mixture turns blue-black. The time, t, is recorded.\n\nExplain why 1/t can be used as a measure of the initial rate of the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The same (small) amount of iodine has formed in every experiment when the colour appears ✓\nSo rate = amount ÷ time ∝ 1/t; very little reactant has been used so this approximates the initial rate ✓\nALLOW the concentrations of reactants change very little before the colour appears\nIGNORE references to the colour of starch alone` },
      { q: `Propanone reacts with iodine in acidic solution:\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + I₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂I(aq) + H⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq)\nThe reaction is catalysed by H⁺(aq). Iodine solution is yellow-brown; all other species are colourless. A colorimeter and the usual laboratory apparatus are available.\n\nDescribe how you would carry out an experiment to determine the orders of reaction with respect to I₂ and to propanone. Explain how you would process your results and how the orders could give information about the rate-determining step.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• Calibrate colorimeter with standard iodine solutions of known concentration to give a calibration curve of absorbance against [I₂]\n• Use a blue / violet filter (complementary to yellow-brown iodine); zero the colorimeter with water / blank\n• Use propanone and H⁺ in large excess so their concentrations stay effectively constant\n• Mix solutions, start timer, record absorbance at regular time intervals at constant temperature (water bath)\n• For propanone: repeat with different initial [propanone], keeping [I₂] and [H⁺] the same, total volume constant\n2. Processing\n• Convert absorbance to [I₂] using the calibration curve\n• Plot [I₂] against time; a straight line (constant gradient) shows zero order with respect to I₂\n• Initial rate from gradient (tangent at t = 0) for each [propanone]\n• Plot initial rate against [propanone]: straight line through origin shows first order; OR doubling [propanone] doubles rate\n3. Rate-determining step\n• Rate equation expected: rate = k[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]\n• I₂ is zero order so I₂ is not involved in the rate-determining step (it reacts in a later, fast step)\n• Propanone (and H⁺) appear in the rate equation so are involved in (or before) the rate-determining step\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide reacts with carbon monoxide above 500 K:\nNO₂(g) + CO(g) → NO(g) + CO₂(g)\nThe rate equation is: rate = k[NO₂]²\n\nSuggest a two-step mechanism for this reaction that is consistent with the rate equation. Identify the rate-determining step.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Step 1: 2NO₂ → NO₃ + NO (slow, rate-determining step) ✓\nStep 2: NO₃ + CO → NO₂ + CO₂ (fast) ✓\nBoth steps must add up to the overall equation\nALLOW other correct intermediates, e.g. NO₂ + NO₂ → N₂O₄ (slow) then N₂O₄ + CO → NO + CO₂ + NO₂\nDO NOT ALLOW a rate-determining step that contains CO` },
      { q: `A student determined the rate constant, k, for a reaction at several temperatures and plotted a graph of ln k against 1/T. The best-fit straight line passes through the points (1/T = 3.00 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, ln k = −5.20) and (1/T = 3.30 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, ln k = −7.90) (data written for this practice question).\nThe Arrhenius equation can be written as ln k = −Eₐ/RT + ln A.\n\nCalculate the activation energy, Eₐ, in kJ mol⁻¹, and the pre-exponential factor, A. Give both answers to 3 significant figures.\nR = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Gradient = (−7.90 − (−5.20)) / (3.30 × 10⁻³ − 3.00 × 10⁻³) = −9000 K ✓\nEₐ = 9000 × 8.314 = 74 826 J mol⁻¹ = 74.8 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nln A = ln k + Eₐ/RT = −5.20 + (9000 × 3.00 × 10⁻³) = 21.8 ✓\nA = e²¹·⁸ = 2.94 × 10⁹ ✓\nALLOW A calculated from the other point (same value)\nECF from incorrect gradient\nIGNORE units of A (same units as k)` },
      { q: `Phosphorus(V) chloride dissociates when heated:\nPCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)\n0.400 mol of PCl₅ was heated in a sealed 1.50 dm³ container. At equilibrium, 0.250 mol of PCl₅ remained (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the value of Kc at this temperature. Give your answer to 3 significant figures and state its units.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Equilibrium amounts: PCl₅ = 0.250 mol; PCl₃ = Cl₂ = 0.400 − 0.250 = 0.150 mol ✓\nConcentrations: [PCl₅] = 0.250/1.50 = 0.167 mol dm⁻³; [PCl₃] = [Cl₂] = 0.150/1.50 = 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ✓\nKc = [PCl₃][Cl₂]/[PCl₅] = (0.100 × 0.100)/0.1667 = 0.0600 ✓\nUnits: mol dm⁻³ ✓\nECF from incorrect equilibrium amounts\nDO NOT ALLOW use of moles without dividing by volume for the second and third marks` },
      { q: `Dinitrogen tetroxide dissociates into nitrogen dioxide:\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)   ΔH = +58 kJ mol⁻¹\n1.00 mol of N₂O₄ was allowed to reach equilibrium at constant temperature and a total pressure of 200 kPa. At equilibrium, 0.400 mol of N₂O₄ had dissociated (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate Kp for this equilibrium, giving your answer to 3 significant figures with units. Predict, with a reason, how the value of Kp would change if the temperature were increased.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Equilibrium moles: N₂O₄ = 0.600 mol; NO₂ = 0.800 mol; total = 1.400 mol ✓\nMole fractions: x(N₂O₄) = 0.429; x(NO₂) = 0.571 ✓\nPartial pressures: p(N₂O₄) = 0.4286 × 200 = 85.7 kPa; p(NO₂) = 0.5714 × 200 = 114.3 kPa ✓\nKp = p(NO₂)² / p(N₂O₄) = 114.3² / 85.7 = 152 kPa ✓\nKp increases because the forward reaction is endothermic (equilibrium shifts right) ✓\nECF at each stage\nALLOW 152.4 kPa\nUnits kPa required for fourth mark` },
      { q: `In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide is oxidised:\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)   ΔH = −197 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nState the effect on the value of Kp of increasing the total pressure at constant temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `No change (Kp depends only on temperature) ✓\nIGNORE references to equilibrium position shifting right` },
      { q: `Ammonia is made in the Haber process:\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nDeduce the units of Kc for this equilibrium.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `dm⁶ mol⁻² ✓\nALLOW mol⁻² dm⁶` },
      { q: `When concentrated nitric acid is mixed with concentrated sulfuric acid, the following equilibrium is set up:\nHNO₃ + H₂SO₄ ⇌ H₂NO₃⁺ + HSO₄⁻\n\nIdentify the two conjugate acid–base pairs in this equilibrium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Pair 1: H₂SO₄ (acid) and HSO₄⁻ (base) ✓\nPair 2: H₂NO₃⁺ (acid) and HNO₃ (base) ✓\nBoth species and their roles needed for each mark\nALLOW acid 1 / base 1 and acid 2 / base 2 labels` },
      { q: `Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH, is a weak acid with Ka = 1.35 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ at 25 °C.\n\nCalculate the pH of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ propanoic acid. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Ka = [H⁺]²/[CH₃CH₂COOH] ✓\n[H⁺] = √(1.35 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.100) = 1.16 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH = −log(1.16 × 10⁻³) = 2.93 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks\nDO NOT ALLOW 2.9 (must be 2 d.p.)` },
      { q: `Barium hydroxide, Ba(OH)₂, is a strong base that dissociates completely in water.\n\nCalculate the pH of 0.0250 mol dm⁻³ Ba(OH)₂(aq) at 25 °C. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.\nKw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0250 = 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ AND [H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0500 = 2.00 × 10⁻¹³ mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH = 12.70 ✓\nALLOW pOH method: pOH = 1.30, pH = 14.00 − 1.30\nALLOW 12.40 for one mark only (ECF from not doubling [OH⁻])` },
      { q: `A buffer solution was prepared by adding 15.0 cm³ of 0.500 mol dm⁻³ NaOH(aq) to 50.0 cm³ of 0.250 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH(aq).\nKa (CH₃COOH) = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³\n\nCalculate the pH of the buffer solution. Give your answer to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(NaOH) = 0.500 × 15.0/1000 = 7.50 × 10⁻³ mol AND n(CH₃COOH) initially = 0.250 × 50.0/1000 = 1.25 × 10⁻² mol ✓\nn(CH₃COO⁻) formed = 7.50 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(CH₃COOH) remaining = 1.25 × 10⁻² − 7.50 × 10⁻³ = 5.00 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\n[H⁺] = Ka × [CH₃COOH]/[CH₃COO⁻] = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 5.00 × 10⁻³/7.50 × 10⁻³ = 1.16 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ ✓\npH = 4.94 ✓\nALLOW 4.93 (from rounding pKa to 4.76 before adding log 1.5)\nALLOW Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]) = 4.76 + 0.18\nECF at each stage\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `The pH of blood is kept close to 7.40 by a buffer system involving carbonic acid, H₂CO₃, and hydrogencarbonate ions, HCO₃⁻:\nH₂CO₃(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq)\n\nExplain how this buffer system controls the pH of blood when small amounts of acid and of alkali enter the blood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Added acid: H⁺ reacts with HCO₃⁻ (conjugate base) OR equilibrium shifts to the left ✓\nAdded alkali: OH⁻ reacts with H⁺ (OR with H₂CO₃: H₂CO₃ + OH⁻ → HCO₃⁻ + H₂O); H₂CO₃ dissociates / equilibrium shifts right to restore [H⁺] ✓\nThe ratio [H₂CO₃]/[HCO₃⁻] changes very little so pH stays (almost) constant ✓\nALLOW H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ → H₂CO₃ for the first mark\nIGNORE references to CO₂ being exhaled` },
      { q: `A student carried out a titration, adding 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH(aq) to 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid. The pH at the equivalence point was 8.7.\nThe pH ranges of three indicators are:\nmethyl orange 3.1–4.4\nbromothymol blue 6.0–7.6\nphenolphthalein 8.3–10.0\n\nJustify which indicator the student should use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Phenolphthalein ✓\nIts pH range lies within the vertical (steep) section of the titration curve / matches the equivalence point pH of 8.7 (weak acid–strong base) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW the second mark if wrong indicator chosen\nALLOW pH range changes colour around pH 8.7` },
      { q: `A student used a pH meter to measure the pH of a series of solutions in a titration of a weak acid with a strong base (PAG11).\n\nDescribe how the student should calibrate the pH meter before use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Rinse electrode with (deionised / distilled) water, then place in a buffer solution of known pH (e.g. pH 7.00) and adjust the meter to read that value ✓\nRinse, then repeat with a second buffer of different known pH (e.g. pH 4.00 or 10.00) ✓\nALLOW buffer solutions of known pH at a stated temperature\nDO NOT ALLOW calibration with water alone` },
      { q: `A student is given a solution of an unknown weak monobasic acid, HA, of approximately 0.1 mol dm⁻³, together with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH(aq), a pH meter and standard laboratory apparatus (PAG11).\n\nDescribe how you would carry out an experiment to determine the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of HA. Your answer should explain how the results would be processed and why the method works.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• Calibrate pH meter with buffer solutions of known pH\n• Pipette 25.0 cm³ of HA into a beaker; place pH probe in the solution; stir (magnetic stirrer)\n• Add NaOH from a burette in small portions (e.g. 1 cm³), recording pH after each addition\n• Add smaller portions (e.g. 0.10–0.20 cm³) near the equivalence point; continue well past it\n• Rinse probe between uses; keep temperature constant as Ka depends on temperature\n2. Processing\n• Plot pH (y-axis) against volume of NaOH added (x-axis)\n• Find the equivalence volume from the centre of the vertical section\n• Find the pH at half the equivalence volume (half-neutralisation)\n• At half-neutralisation pH = pKa; Ka = 10⁻ᵖᴴ\n3. Explanation\n• At half-neutralisation, half of HA has been converted to A⁻, so [HA] = [A⁻]\n• Ka = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA] simplifies to Ka = [H⁺]\n• Alternative: measure pH of HA of known concentration, Ka = [H⁺]²/[HA], assuming [HA]eqm ≈ [HA]initial and negligible ionisation of water – less reliable\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Hydrogen chloride gas reacts with ammonia gas to form a white solid:\nHCl(g) + NH₃(g) → NH₄Cl(s)\nIn this reaction HCl acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid.\n\nState what is meant by a Brønsted–Lowry acid.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `A proton (H⁺) donor ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW hydrogen donor` },
      { q: `The table gives enthalpy changes for calcium and chlorine.\n\nEnthalpy of formation of CaCl₂(s): −795 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy of atomisation of calcium: +178 kJ mol⁻¹\nFirst ionisation energy of calcium: +590 kJ mol⁻¹\nSecond ionisation energy of calcium: +1145 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy of atomisation of chlorine (½Cl₂ → Cl): +122 kJ mol⁻¹\nFirst electron affinity of chlorine: −349 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nUse a Born–Haber cycle to calculate the lattice enthalpy of calcium chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Correct doubling: 2 × (+122) = +244 AND 2 × (−349) = −698 ✓\nΔLEH = ΔfH − (ΔatH(Ca) + IE₁ + IE₂ + 2ΔatH(Cl) + 2EA) = −795 − (178 + 590 + 1145 + 244 − 698) ✓\nΔLEH = −2254 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW −2250 kJ mol⁻¹\nALLOW ECF for one error (e.g. −1981 from not doubling Cl values scores 2 marks)\nDO NOT ALLOW +2254 for the final mark` },
      { q: `Potassium chloride dissolves in water. The following data are available.\n\nLattice enthalpy of KCl: −711 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of hydration of K⁺: −322 kJ mol⁻¹\nEnthalpy change of hydration of Cl⁻: −363 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of potassium chloride.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔsolH = −ΔLEH + ΔhydH(K⁺) + ΔhydH(Cl⁻) = +711 + (−322) + (−363) ✓\nΔsolH = +26 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for −1396 kJ mol⁻¹ (lattice enthalpy sign not reversed)\nSign required for the second mark` },
      { q: `The lattice enthalpies of two ionic compounds are:\nNaCl: −787 kJ mol⁻¹\nMgO: −3791 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain why the lattice enthalpy of MgO is much more exothermic than that of NaCl.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ have greater (ionic) charges than Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ✓\nMg²⁺ is smaller than Na⁺ AND O²⁻ is smaller than Cl⁻ OR ions in MgO have greater charge density ✓\nStronger (electrostatic) attraction between oppositely charged ions in MgO ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW references to atoms or molecules instead of ions\nIGNORE stronger ionic bonds without reference to attraction` },
      { q: `A student determined the enthalpy change of solution of ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃. 4.00 g of NH₄NO₃ was dissolved in 50.0 g of water in a polystyrene cup. The temperature of the solution fell by 6.1 °C (data written for this practice question).\nSpecific heat capacity of the solution = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹; Mr(NH₄NO₃) = 80.0\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of NH₄NO₃, in kJ mol⁻¹. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `q = mcΔT = 50.0 × 4.18 × 6.1 = 1275 J ✓\nn(NH₄NO₃) = 4.00/80.0 = 0.0500 mol ✓\nΔsolH = +1.275/0.0500 = +25.5 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nSign required for the third mark (temperature falls, so endothermic)\nALLOW use of 54.0 g: +27.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nECF from incorrect q or n` },
      { q: `Ammonium chloride decomposes on heating:\nNH₄Cl(s) → NH₃(g) + HCl(g)\n\nPredict, with a reason, the sign of the entropy change, ΔS, for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Positive AND solid forms (two moles of) gas / number of gas moles increases (0 → 2) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW positive with no reason` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate decomposes when heated strongly:\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)   ΔH = +178 kJ mol⁻¹\nStandard entropies, S / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹: CaCO₃(s) 92.9; CaO(s) 39.7; CO₂(g) 213.6\n\nCalculate the entropy change, ΔS, for this reaction and the minimum temperature, in K, at which the decomposition becomes feasible. Give the temperature to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔS = (39.7 + 213.6) − 92.9 = +160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nFeasible when ΔG ≤ 0, so T = ΔH/ΔS ✓\nConversion of units: ΔH = 178 000 J mol⁻¹ OR ΔS = 0.1604 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ ✓\nT = 178 000/160.4 = 1110 K ✓\nALLOW 1109.7 K\nECF from incorrect ΔS\nDO NOT ALLOW 1.11 K (units not converted) for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `Ammonia is formed from its elements:\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g)   ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹   ΔS = −198.8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\nA mixture of nitrogen and hydrogen at room temperature shows no detectable formation of ammonia.\n\nCalculate ΔG at 298 K and explain why no ammonia forms at room temperature.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = −92 − 298 × (−0.1988) ✓\nΔG = −32.8 kJ mol⁻¹ (so the reaction is feasible) ✓\nThe activation energy is very high, so the rate is too slow at 298 K / ΔG does not take kinetics into account ✓\nALLOW −32.76 to −33 kJ mol⁻¹\nALLOW −32 800 J mol⁻¹\nECF for a ΔG value from an incorrect unit conversion for the first mark only` },
      { q: `Acidified dichromate(VI) ions can be reduced to chromium(III) ions by zinc metal.\nCr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + 14H⁺(aq) + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O(l)\nZn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻\n\nWrite the overall equation for this reaction and state the colour change seen in the solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 3Zn → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 3Zn²⁺ ✓\nOrange to green ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW multiples\nIGNORE further reduction to blue Cr²⁺ if mentioned as a later change` },
      { q: `A student determined the percentage of copper in a sample of brass. 1.50 g of brass was dissolved in nitric acid. The solution was neutralised and made up to 250.0 cm³ with deionised water. 25.0 cm³ portions were pipetted into a conical flask, excess potassium iodide was added and the iodine formed was titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium thiosulfate. The mean titre was 16.40 cm³ (data written for this practice question).\n2Cu²⁺(aq) + 4I⁻(aq) → 2CuI(s) + I₂(aq)\nI₂(aq) + 2S₂O₃²⁻(aq) → 2I⁻(aq) + S₄O₆²⁻(aq)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of copper in the brass. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(S₂O₃²⁻) = 0.100 × 16.40/1000 = 1.64 × 10⁻³ mol ✓\nn(Cu²⁺) in 25.0 cm³ = n(S₂O₃²⁻) = 1.64 × 10⁻³ mol (2Cu²⁺ ≡ I₂ ≡ 2S₂O₃²⁻) ✓\nn(Cu²⁺) in 250.0 cm³ = 1.64 × 10⁻² mol ✓\nmass of Cu = 1.64 × 10⁻² × 63.5 = 1.04 g ✓\n% Cu = 1.0414/1.50 × 100 = 69.4% ✓\nECF at each stage\nALLOW Ar(Cu) = 63.5 or 63.6 (gives 69.5%)\nCorrect answer with no working scores all marks` },
      { q: `A student titrated iodine solution with sodium thiosulfate solution. Starch indicator was added only when the solution in the conical flask had turned pale yellow.\n\nExplain why the starch was added near the end point rather than at the start, and state the colour change at the end point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `If added at the start, a large amount of iodine would bind to starch (forming a complex that releases iodine slowly), making the end point inaccurate / less sharp ✓\nBlue-black to colourless ✓\nALLOW starch–iodine complex is hard to break down / insoluble at high [I₂]\nDO NOT ALLOW 'clear' for colourless` },
      { q: `In a redox titration, a student recorded a titre of 16.40 cm³ from a burette. Each burette reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the titre.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(2 × 0.05)/16.40 × 100 = 0.61% ✓\nALLOW 0.6%\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.30% (one reading only)` },
      { q: `A student wanted to measure the standard electrode potential of the Cu²⁺/Cu half-cell by connecting it to a standard hydrogen electrode (PAG8).\n\nDescribe the standard hydrogen electrode, including the standard conditions used.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `H₂ gas bubbled over a platinum electrode ✓\nIn a solution with [H⁺] = 1 mol dm⁻³ (e.g. 1 mol dm⁻³ HCl) ✓\nH₂ at 100 kPa pressure AND temperature 298 K ✓\nALLOW 1 atm / 101 kPa for pressure\nALLOW 25 °C\nDO NOT ALLOW 1 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄ (gives 2 mol dm⁻³ H⁺)` },
      { q: `Standard electrode potentials:\nCl₂(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ 2Cl⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +1.36 V\nFe³⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ Fe²⁺(aq)   E⦵ = +0.77 V\nI₂(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ 2I⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +0.54 V\n\nPredict whether chlorine and iodine will each oxidise Fe²⁺ ions. Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Cl₂ will oxidise Fe²⁺ because E⦵(Cl₂/Cl⁻) is more positive than E⦵(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) ✓\nI₂ will not oxidise Fe²⁺ because E⦵(I₂/I⁻) is less positive than E⦵(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) ✓\nALLOW Ecell = +0.59 V (positive, feasible) and Ecell = −0.23 V (negative, not feasible)` },
      { q: `An alkaline hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell uses the following half-cells:\n2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ ⇌ H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq)   E⦵ = −0.83 V\nO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻ ⇌ 4OH⁻(aq)   E⦵ = +0.40 V\n\nWrite the half-equations for the reactions at the negative and positive electrodes when the cell is supplying electricity, write the overall cell reaction and calculate the standard cell potential.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Negative electrode: H₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2H₂O + 2e⁻ ✓\nPositive electrode: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻ ✓\nOverall: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O ✓\nE⦵cell = 0.40 − (−0.83) = +1.23 V ✓\nALLOW multiples\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW equilibrium arrows in the half-equations at each electrode` },
      { q: `A student set up an electrochemical cell from a Cu²⁺(aq)/Cu(s) half-cell and a Zn²⁺(aq)/Zn(s) half-cell, connected by a high-resistance voltmeter (PAG8).\n\nState what is used to connect the two solutions and complete the circuit.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `A salt bridge, e.g. filter paper soaked in saturated KNO₃(aq) ✓\nALLOW any unreactive ionic solution, e.g. KNO₃ or NaNO₃\nDO NOT ALLOW KCl if Ag⁺ present; DO NOT ALLOW a wire` },
      { q: `A student measured the cell potentials of three cells, each formed from a different half-cell connected to a Cu²⁺(aq)/Cu(s) half-cell (E⦵ = +0.34 V). All solutions were 1.00 mol dm⁻³ and the temperature was 298 K (data written for this practice question).\n\nHalf-cell X: Zn²⁺(aq)/Zn(s) – cell potential 1.10 V, Zn electrode negative\nHalf-cell Y: Ag⁺(aq)/Ag(s) – cell potential 0.46 V, Ag electrode positive\nHalf-cell Z: Fe³⁺(aq), Fe²⁺(aq)/Pt(s) – cell potential 0.43 V, Pt electrode positive\n\nDescribe how the student would set up cell Z and measure its potential. Use the results to deduce the standard electrode potentials of X, Y and Z, and evaluate what the values show about the reactions that are feasible between these species.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Method\n• Cu rod (cleaned with emery paper) in 1.00 mol dm⁻³ Cu²⁺(aq)\n• Pt electrode in a solution containing both Fe³⁺ and Fe²⁺, each 1.00 mol dm⁻³ (equal concentrations)\n• Salt bridge of filter paper soaked in KNO₃(aq)\n• High-resistance voltmeter so that negligible current flows; 298 K\n2. Deducing E⦵ values\n• X: E⦵ = 0.34 − 1.10 = −0.76 V (Zn is negative electrode, so less positive than Cu)\n• Y: E⦵ = 0.34 + 0.46 = +0.80 V\n• Z: E⦵ = 0.34 + 0.43 = +0.77 V\n• Order of oxidising power: Ag⁺ greater than Fe³⁺ greater than Cu²⁺ greater than Zn²⁺\n3. Feasibility\n• Fe³⁺ will oxidise Cu: 2Fe³⁺ + Cu → 2Fe²⁺ + Cu²⁺ (Ecell = +0.43 V)\n• Zn reduces Cu²⁺, Fe³⁺ and Ag⁺; a Zn/Ag cell would give Ecell = 0.80 − (−0.76) = 1.56 V\n• Ag⁺ + Fe²⁺ → Ag + Fe³⁺ predicted feasible but Ecell only +0.03 V, so may not occur under non-standard conditions\n• Limitations: E⦵ predicts feasibility only, not rate; non-standard concentrations change electrode potentials\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Chromium and copper are d-block elements whose atoms have electron configurations that do not follow the expected pattern of sub-shell filling.\n\nWrite the full electron configurations of a chromium atom and a Cu²⁺ ion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cr: 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁵4s¹ ✓\nCu²⁺: 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶3d⁹ ✓\nALLOW 4s before 3d in the written order\nDO NOT ALLOW [Ar] shorthand (full configuration asked for)` },
      { q: `Scandium is in the d block of the Periodic Table but is not classed as a transition element. Its only ion is Sc³⁺.\n\nExplain why scandium is not a transition element.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Sc³⁺ has an empty d sub-shell (3d⁰) / Sc forms no ion with an incomplete d sub-shell ✓\nALLOW Sc³⁺ is [Ar] / 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶` },
      { q: `Cisplatin is an anticancer drug with the formula Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂. It is one of two stereoisomers of this formula.\n\nDraw 3-D diagrams of the two stereoisomers, identify which is cisplatin, and state how cisplatin acts as an anticancer drug.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Two square planar structures: cis with both Cl adjacent (90°) and trans with Cl opposite (180°) ✓\nCis isomer identified as cisplatin ✓\nCisplatin binds to DNA (via N of guanine bases) in cancer cells, preventing cell division / DNA replication ✓\nALLOW bonds from Pt to N of NH₃ shown clearly\nDO NOT ALLOW tetrahedral structures` },
      { q: `Ethane-1,2-diamine, H₂NCH₂CH₂NH₂ (en), is a bidentate ligand. It forms the complex ion [Ni(en)₃]²⁺, which shows optical isomerism.\n\nDraw 3-D diagrams of the two optical isomers of [Ni(en)₃]²⁺.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Octahedral complex with three en ligands, each bonding at two adjacent positions (ALLOW N–N arcs for en) ✓\nSecond structure drawn as non-superimposable mirror image of the first ✓\nCharge 2+ required on at least one structure\nALLOW correct wedges/dashes` },
      { q: `A student added reagents to separate samples of aqueous copper(II) sulfate, which contains [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq).\nSample 1: excess concentrated aqueous ammonia was added.\nSample 2: excess concentrated hydrochloric acid was added.\n\nFor each sample, write an equation for the ligand substitution and state the colour of the final solution. Give the shape of the copper complex formed in sample 2.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O ✓\nDeep / dark blue solution ✓\n[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O ✓\nYellow solution AND tetrahedral ✓\nALLOW ⇌ in place of →\nIGNORE pale blue precipitate seen first with ammonia\nDO NOT ALLOW green for [CuCl₄]²⁻` },
      { q: `In red blood cells, haemoglobin contains Fe²⁺ ions that bind oxygen as a ligand. Carbon monoxide is toxic.\n\nExplain why carbon monoxide is toxic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CO is a ligand that replaces O₂ / binds to Fe²⁺ in haemoglobin (ligand substitution) ✓\nCO binds more strongly than O₂ (essentially irreversibly) so less oxygen is transported ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW CO reacts with haemoglobin without reference to binding/substitution` },
      { q: `Four unlabelled solutions, A to D, each contain one of the following cations: Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺, Fe³⁺, Cr³⁺. A student added aqueous sodium hydroxide, then excess, to each solution.\n\nA: orange-brown precipitate, insoluble in excess\nB: grey-green precipitate, dissolves in excess to form a dark green solution\nC: green precipitate that turns brown at the surface on standing, insoluble in excess\nD: blue precipitate, insoluble in excess\n\nIdentify the cation in each solution and write an ionic equation, with state symbols, for the formation of the precipitate in solution A.`, marks: 4, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A = Fe³⁺ AND C = Fe²⁺ ✓\nB = Cr³⁺ ✓\nD = Cu²⁺ ✓\nFe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s) ✓\nALLOW [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(H₂O)₃(s) + 3H₂O(l)\nState symbols required for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `When copper(I) oxide is added to warm dilute sulfuric acid, a brown solid and a blue solution form. In aqueous solution, Cu⁺ ions disproportionate.\n\nWrite an ionic equation for the disproportionation of Cu⁺(aq).`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2Cu⁺(aq) → Cu(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) ✓\nIGNORE state symbols\nALLOW Cu₂O + 2H⁺ → Cu + Cu²⁺ + H₂O` },
      { q: `A pale green crystalline solid, X, is a hydrated salt of a single transition metal cation and a single anion. A student carried out the following tests (data written for this practice question).\n\nTest 1: aqueous sodium hydroxide added to a solution of X – green precipitate formed, which turned brown on standing in air.\nTest 2: dilute hydrochloric acid then aqueous barium chloride added to a solution of X – white precipitate.\nTest 3: 2.78 g of X was dissolved in dilute sulfuric acid and made up to 250.0 cm³. 25.0 cm³ portions were titrated with 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ KMnO₄(aq). The mean titre was 10.00 cm³.\nMnO₄⁻(aq) + 8H⁺(aq) + 5Fe²⁺(aq) → Mn²⁺(aq) + 4H₂O(l) + 5Fe³⁺(aq)\n\nUse all of the evidence to determine the formula of X, including its water of crystallisation. Explain your reasoning, including what is seen at the end point of the titration.`, marks: 6, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Cation\n• Green precipitate is Fe(OH)₂, so cation is Fe²⁺: Fe²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂\n• Turns brown because Fe(OH)₂ is oxidised by air to Fe(OH)₃ (Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺)\n• Fe²⁺ confirmed as it reduces MnO₄⁻ in Test 3\n2. Anion\n• White precipitate with BaCl₂ is BaSO₄, so anion is SO₄²⁻: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄\n• HCl added first to remove carbonate (which would also give a white precipitate)\n• Salt is FeSO₄·xH₂O\n3. Titration and formula\n• n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.0200 × 10.00/1000 = 2.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol\n• n(Fe²⁺) in 25.0 cm³ = 5 × 2.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol; in 250.0 cm³ = 1.00 × 10⁻² mol\n• M(X) = 2.78/1.00 × 10⁻² = 278 g mol⁻¹; M(FeSO₄) = 151.9, so mass of water = 126.1, x = 126.1/18.0 = 7\n• X = FeSO₄·7H₂O\n• End point: first permanent pale pink colour (MnO₄⁻ in excess); no indicator needed as MnO₄⁻ is self-indicating\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student is given a solution that may contain carbonate, sulfate and chloride ions. The student plans to test for all three anions on the same sample, using dilute nitric acid, aqueous barium nitrate, aqueous silver nitrate and limewater.\n\nDescribe the sequence of tests the student should carry out, with the expected observations for a positive result, and explain why the tests must be done in this order.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Carbonate first: add dilute HNO₃ – effervescence; gas turns limewater milky ✓\nSulfate next: add Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) – white precipitate (BaSO₄) ✓\nHalide last: add AgNO₃(aq) – white precipitate (AgCl), soluble in dilute NH₃ ✓\nOrder: carbonate must be removed first as BaCO₃ and Ag₂CO₃ also form precipitates; sulfate tested before halide as Ag₂SO₄ can precipitate ✓\nALLOW filter off BaSO₄ before adding AgNO₃\nDO NOT ALLOW use of HCl or BaCl₂ (adds Cl⁻)` }
    ]
  },
  'M6': {
    name: 'Organic Chemistry and Analysis',
    questions: [
      { q: `The enthalpy change of hydrogenation of cyclohexene, C₆H₁₀, is −120 kJ mol⁻¹:\nC₆H₁₀ + H₂ → C₆H₁₂   ΔH = −120 kJ mol⁻¹\nThe enthalpy change of hydrogenation of benzene to cyclohexane is −208 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nExplain how these data provide evidence against the Kekulé model of benzene.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Kekulé model has three C=C bonds so predicted ΔH(hydrogenation) = 3 × (−120) = −360 kJ mol⁻¹ ✓\nActual value is 152 kJ mol⁻¹ less exothermic, so benzene is more stable than the Kekulé structure ✓\nStability is due to delocalised π electrons / π-system spread over all six carbon atoms (not localised C=C bonds) ✓\nALLOW less energy released than expected for 3 C=C bonds for M2\nALLOW 'more energy needed to break bonds in benzene' for M2\nIGNORE references to bond lengths` },
      { q: `Bromobenzene is made by reacting benzene with bromine in the presence of iron(III) bromide, FeBr₃, which acts as a halogen carrier.\n\nWrite equations to show how FeBr₃ generates the electrophile and how FeBr₃ is regenerated at the end of the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Br₂ + FeBr₃ → Br⁺ + FeBr₄⁻ ✓\nH⁺ + FeBr₄⁻ → FeBr₃ + HBr ✓\nALLOW AlBr₃ / Fe in place of FeBr₃ if consistent\nIGNORE state symbols\nDO NOT ALLOW equations producing Br⁻ as electrophile` },
      { q: `Ethylbenzene is an intermediate in the manufacture of poly(phenylethene). It is made by reacting benzene with chloroethane in the presence of aluminium chloride, AlCl₃.\n\nState the name of this type of reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Friedel–Crafts alkylation ✓\nALLOW electrophilic substitution\nIGNORE alkylation alone\nDO NOT ALLOW Friedel–Crafts acylation` },
      { q: `Phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃, is used in fragrances. It can be made from benzene in a one-step reaction.\n\nState the reagent and catalyst needed and write an equation to show the formation of the electrophile.`, marks: 3, command: 'State', markScheme: `Reagent: ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl ✓\nCatalyst: AlCl₃ (anhydrous) ✓\nCH₃COCl + AlCl₃ → CH₃CO⁺ + AlCl₄⁻ ✓\nALLOW FeCl₃ as catalyst\nALLOW CH₃C⁺O in equation\nIGNORE 'halogen carrier' as a name for the catalyst\nDO NOT ALLOW ethanoic acid as reagent` },
      { q: `Nitrobenzene is made by warming benzene to 50 °C with a mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nWrite an equation for the formation of the electrophile and outline the mechanism for the reaction of benzene with this electrophile. Show curly arrows and relevant charges.`, marks: 4, command: 'Outline', diagram: true, markScheme: `HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ✓\nCurly arrow from the delocalised ring (π electrons) to N of NO₂⁺ ✓\nCorrect intermediate: broken delocalised ring (horseshoe over five carbons), + charge inside, and the sp³ carbon carrying both H and NO₂ ✓\nCurly arrow from the C–H bond back into the ring to reform the delocalised ring, giving nitrobenzene + H⁺ ✓\nALLOW HNO₃ + 2H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + 2HSO₄⁻ + H₃O⁺\nALLOW horseshoe that covers at least three carbons with the gap towards the tetrahedral carbon\nDO NOT ALLOW Kekulé structures for the reactant\nIGNORE regeneration of H₂SO₄ from H⁺ + HSO₄⁻` },
      { q: `A student adds bromine water dropwise to aqueous phenol at room temperature. No halogen carrier is used. Benzene does not react with bromine under these conditions.\n\nState what the student observes, write an equation for the reaction, and explain why phenol reacts with bromine more readily than benzene does.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Bromine water decolourised AND white precipitate forms ✓\nC₆H₅OH + 3Br₂ → C₆H₂Br₃OH + 3HBr ✓\nA lone pair (p-orbital) on oxygen is (partially) delocalised into the ring, increasing the π-electron density ✓\nThe electron-rich ring induces a dipole in / polarises Br₂ (so no halogen carrier needed); benzene's electron density is too low to polarise Br₂ ✓\nALLOW orange to colourless for decolourised\nALLOW structural formula of 2,4,6-tribromophenol\nDO NOT ALLOW 'lone pair delocalised' without reference to oxygen\nDO NOT ALLOW 'benzene is stable' alone` },
      { q: `A student is given two colourless aqueous solutions, one of phenol and one of ethanoic acid. Both are weak acids and both react with aqueous sodium hydroxide.\n\nDescribe a test that the student could use to distinguish the two solutions. Include the observations for each.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add sodium carbonate (aq) / Na₂CO₃ ✓\nEthanoic acid: effervescence / bubbles (of CO₂); phenol: no effervescence ✓\nALLOW NaHCO₃ / any named carbonate or hydrogencarbonate\nALLOW 'phenol is too weak an acid to react with carbonate' as reason\nDO NOT ALLOW indicator tests\nM2 dependent on M1` },
      { q: `Phenol reacts with dilute nitric acid at room temperature to form a mixture of two isomeric products with molecular formula C₆H₅NO₃.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction and name the two organic products.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₆H₅OH + HNO₃ → C₆H₄(OH)NO₂ + H₂O ✓\n2-nitrophenol AND 4-nitrophenol ✓\nALLOW molecular formulae in equation: C₆H₆O + HNO₃ → C₆H₅NO₃ + H₂O\nDO NOT ALLOW 3-nitrophenol\nIGNORE state symbols` },
      { q: `Groups already attached to a benzene ring affect where a second substituent goes. –OH and –NH₂ groups direct to the 2- and 4- positions; –NO₂ directs to the 3- position.\n\nPredict the organic product of each reaction:\n• nitrobenzene with bromine and FeBr₃\n• phenylamine with excess bromine water`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene / 3-bromonitrobenzene ✓\n2,4,6-tribromophenylamine ✓\nALLOW correct structures\nALLOW 2,4,6-tribromoaniline\nDO NOT ALLOW 2- or 4-bromonitrobenzene` },
      { q: `A chemist plans to make 1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene from benzene in two steps. A bromine atom on a benzene ring directs further substitution to the 2- and 4- positions. A nitro group directs to the 3- position.\n\nExplain, with reagents for each step, the order in which the chemist must carry out the two steps.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Step 1: nitration first, using concentrated HNO₃ and concentrated H₂SO₄ ✓\nStep 2: bromination using Br₂ with a halogen carrier (FeBr₃ / AlBr₃ / Fe) ✓\n–NO₂ is 3-directing so Br substitutes at the 3-position; brominating first would give 2- and 4-substituted products ✓\nALLOW temperature of 50 °C (IGNORE if missing)\nDO NOT ALLOW bromine water for step 2` },
      { q: `Propanone is reduced to propan-2-ol by aqueous sodium borohydride, NaBH₄. The reaction is a nucleophilic addition in which NaBH₄ is a source of hydride ions, H⁻.\n\nOutline the mechanism for this reaction. Show curly arrows, relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Outline', diagram: true, markScheme: `Dipole on C=O (Cδ+, Oδ−) AND curly arrow from lone pair of H⁻ to the carbonyl carbon ✓\nCurly arrow from C=O π bond to oxygen ✓\nCorrect intermediate: (CH₃)₂CH–O⁻ with lone pair on O ✓\nCurly arrow from lone pair on O⁻ to H of H₂O AND curly arrow from O–H bond to O of water, giving (CH₃)₂CHOH + OH⁻ ✓\nALLOW H⁺ in place of H₂O for M4 (arrow from O⁻ lone pair to H⁺)\nDO NOT ALLOW arrow from NaBH₄ or from the B atom\nIGNORE Na⁺` },
      { q: `But-2-enal, CH₃CH=CHCHO, is reduced separately using two different reducing agents: NaBH₄(aq), and hydrogen with a nickel catalyst.\n\nWrite an equation for each reduction. Use [H] to represent the reducing agent where appropriate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH=CHCHO + 2[H] → CH₃CH=CHCH₂OH ✓\nCH₃CH=CHCHO + 2H₂ → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH ✓\nALLOW skeletal / displayed formulae\nM1: C=C must be retained (NaBH₄ does not reduce C=C)\nDO NOT ALLOW 4[H] for M1` },
      { q: `Propanone reacts with HCN, generated from NaCN and a dilute acid, to form a hydroxynitrile.\n\nState the reagents, outline the mechanism and name the organic product. Show curly arrows, relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 5, command: 'Outline', diagram: true, markScheme: `Reagents: NaCN(aq) AND H₂SO₄(aq) / H⁺(aq) ✓\nDipole on C=O AND curly arrow from lone pair on C of ⁻CN to the carbonyl carbon ✓\nCurly arrow from C=O π bond to O AND correct intermediate (CH₃)₂C(CN)O⁻ ✓\nCurly arrow from lone pair on O⁻ to H⁺ (or to H of H₂O / HCN) ✓\nProduct name: 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile ✓\nALLOW KCN\nDO NOT ALLOW arrow from N of ⁻CN in M2\nIGNORE Na⁺` },
      { q: `Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO, reacts with NaCN(aq) and H₂SO₄(aq).\n\nName the organic product.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `2-hydroxybutanenitrile ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 2-hydroxypropanenitrile / 1-hydroxybutanenitrile` },
      { q: `Ethanal reacts with HCN to form 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN. The product has no effect on plane-polarised light.\n\nExplain why the product is optically inactive.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The C=O group / carbonyl carbon is planar (trigonal planar) ✓\n⁻CN can attack from either side (above or below the plane) with equal probability ✓\nA 50:50 mixture of the two optical isomers (racemic mixture) forms, so the rotations cancel ✓\nALLOW 'equal amounts of each enantiomer'\nDO NOT ALLOW 'no chiral centre'` },
      { q: `Three unlabelled bottles contain butanal, butanone and butan-2-ol. A student has 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP) and Tollens' reagent.\n\nDescribe how the student could use these two reagents to identify each compound. Include the observations.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Add 2,4-DNP: orange / yellow precipitate with butanal AND butanone; no change with butan-2-ol ✓\nWarm the two carbonyl compounds with Tollens' reagent (in a water bath) ✓\nButanal gives a silver mirror / grey-black precipitate; butanone no change ✓\nALLOW Tollens' used first on all three, with 2,4-DNP then distinguishing butanone from butan-2-ol\nDO NOT ALLOW 'red precipitate' for 2,4-DNP\nIGNORE 'stays colourless' for no change` },
      { q: `Solubility of some carboxylic acids in water at 20 °C (data written for this practice question):\n| Acid | Formula | Solubility / g per 100 g water |\n| ethanoic acid | CH₃COOH | miscible |\n| butanoic acid | CH₃(CH₂)₂COOH | miscible |\n| hexanoic acid | CH₃(CH₂)₄COOH | 1.0 |\n| octanoic acid | CH₃(CH₂)₆COOH | 0.07 |\n\nExplain the trend shown in the table.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The COOH group forms hydrogen bonds with water molecules ✓\nAs the hydrocarbon chain gets longer, the larger non-polar part cannot hydrogen bond / disrupts hydrogen bonding with water so solubility decreases ✓\nALLOW 'polar COOH' for M1 only if hydrogen bonding is mentioned\nIGNORE references to London forces between acid molecules` },
      { q: `Kettle descaler contains ethanoic acid. It removes limescale, CaCO₃(s).\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of ethanoic acid with calcium carbonate and state one observation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `2CH₃COOH + CaCO₃ → (CH₃COO)₂Ca + CO₂ + H₂O ✓\nEffervescence / fizzing / solid dissolves ✓\nALLOW Ca(CH₃COO)₂\nDO NOT ALLOW 'gas produced' or 'CO₂ produced' as an observation` },
      { q: `Banana flavouring contains an ester made by heating 3-methylbutan-1-ol, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂OH, with ethanoic acid and a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nName the ester.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `3-methylbutyl ethanoate ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW ethyl 3-methylbutanoate` },
      { q: `A student heats methyl benzoate, C₆H₅COOCH₃, under reflux with aqueous sodium hydroxide. After cooling, the student adds dilute hydrochloric acid and a white solid forms.\n\nWrite an equation for the hydrolysis and explain why the student used NaOH(aq) rather than an acid catalyst, and why the white solid forms on adding HCl(aq).`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `C₆H₅COOCH₃ + NaOH → C₆H₅COONa + CH₃OH ✓\nAlkaline hydrolysis goes to completion / is not reversible, whereas acid hydrolysis is an equilibrium (so higher yield) ✓\nHCl protonates the benzoate ion to form benzoic acid, which is insoluble / only slightly soluble in water ✓\nALLOW ionic: C₆H₅COOCH₃ + OH⁻ → C₆H₅COO⁻ + CH₃OH\nALLOW C₆H₅COO⁻ + H⁺ → C₆H₅COOH for M3` },
      { q: `A few drops of ethanoyl chloride are added to water in a fume cupboard. A vigorous reaction takes place.\n\nState one observation and write an equation for the reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Misty / steamy fumes ✓\nCH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl ✓\nALLOW 'white fumes'\nDO NOT ALLOW 'gas produced' or 'HCl given off' as the observation` },
      { q: `Phenyl ethanoate, CH₃COOC₆H₅, can be made from phenol and ethanoyl chloride at room temperature. The ester cannot be made in good yield by heating phenol with ethanoic acid and an acid catalyst.\n\nWrite an equation for the preparation from ethanoyl chloride and suggest two reasons why ethanoyl chloride is used rather than ethanoic acid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `CH₃COCl + C₆H₅OH → CH₃COOC₆H₅ + HCl ✓\nAcyl chloride reaction is not reversible / goes to completion, so higher yield ✓\nAcyl chloride is more reactive so the reaction is fast at room temperature (phenol reacts too slowly with carboxylic acids) ✓\nIGNORE 'no catalyst needed' unless linked to reactivity` },
      { q: `Propanoyl chloride, CH₃CH₂COCl, reacts with excess methylamine, CH₃NH₂, to form an amide.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction and classify the amide formed as primary or secondary.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂COCl + 2CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CH₂CONHCH₃ + CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻ ✓\nSecondary amide ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH₂COCl + CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CH₂CONHCH₃ + HCl\nALLOW CH₃NH₃Cl\nALLOW N-methylpropanamide named instead of formula in equation` },
      { q: `The order of base strength of three nitrogen compounds is:\nethylamine, C₂H₅NH₂ (strongest)  ammonia, NH₃  phenylamine, C₆H₅NH₂ (weakest)\n\nExplain this order.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Base strength depends on how readily the lone pair on N accepts a proton (H⁺) ✓\nEthyl group is electron-donating / pushes electron density onto N, so the lone pair is more available than in NH₃ ✓\nIn phenylamine the N lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring, so it is less available ✓\nALLOW 'electron density on N' throughout\nDO NOT ALLOW 'electronegativity' arguments` },
      { q: `Ethylamine is made by heating bromoethane with an excess of ammonia dissolved in ethanol.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction and explain why excess ammonia is used.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br ✓\nExcess NH₃ reduces further substitution (forming secondary/tertiary amines or quaternary ammonium salts) ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH₂Br + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HBr\nALLOW 'increases yield of primary amine' with reason` },
      { q: `Phenylamine is made in the laboratory from nitrobenzene in two stages. In stage 1, nitrobenzene is reduced. In stage 2, excess NaOH(aq) is added to the reaction mixture.\n\nState the reagents for stage 1, write an equation for the reduction using [H], and explain why NaOH(aq) is added in stage 2.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Tin AND concentrated hydrochloric acid (heat under reflux) ✓\nC₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O ✓\nIn acid the amine is protonated as the phenylammonium ion / salt; NaOH removes H⁺ to release free phenylamine ✓\nALLOW Sn/HCl(conc)\nALLOW C₆H₅NH₃⁺ + OH⁻ → C₆H₅NH₂ + H₂O for M3\nDO NOT ALLOW 'NaOH neutralises the acid' alone for M3` },
      { q: `Phenylamine is only slightly soluble in water but dissolves in dilute hydrochloric acid.\n\nGive the formula of the salt formed.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻ ✓\nALLOW C₆H₅NH₃Cl\nALLOW phenylammonium chloride` },
      { q: `Alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, is an α-amino acid.\n\nDraw the structures of the organic products formed when alanine reacts with (i) excess NaOH(aq) and (ii) excess HCl(aq).`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `(i) CH₃CH(NH₂)COO⁻ Na⁺ ✓\n(ii) CH₃CH(NH₃⁺)COOH (Cl⁻) ✓\nALLOW COONa and NH₃Cl\nDO NOT ALLOW NH₃⁺ in (i) or COO⁻ in (ii)` },
      { q: `Alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, is heated with methanol in the presence of concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nDraw the structure of the organic product.`, marks: 1, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `CH₃CH(NH₂)COOCH₃ ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH(NH₃⁺)COOCH₃ (amine protonated by the acid)\nDO NOT ALLOW methyl ester at the NH₂ group` },
      { q: `Threonine is an essential amino acid with the structure CH₃CH(OH)CH(NH₂)COOH.\n\nState the number of chiral centres in a threonine molecule.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `2 ✓\nALLOW the two chiral carbons correctly identified (C2 and C3)` },
      { q: `2-Hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid), CH₃CH(OH)COOH, is formed in muscles during anaerobic respiration. It exists as two optical isomers.\n\nDraw 3D diagrams of the two optical isomers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `One correct 3D structure: chiral carbon with H, OH, CH₃ and COOH using wedge and dashed bonds (two in plane, one wedge, one dash) ✓\nSecond structure is the mirror image of the first ✓\nALLOW any two groups swapped as the second isomer\nDO NOT ALLOW structures without 3D bonds\nM2 dependent on M1` },
      { q: `Poly(lactic acid) is a biodegradable polyester made from 2-hydroxypropanoic acid, CH₃CH(OH)COOH. Nylon-6,6 is a polyamide made from hexane-1,6-diamine, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂, and hexanedioyl dichloride, ClOC(CH₂)₄COCl.\n\nDraw one repeat unit of each polymer and state the small molecule eliminated in each polymerisation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Poly(lactic acid) repeat unit: –O–CH(CH₃)–CO– with ester link shown and trailing bonds ✓\nSmall molecule: H₂O ✓\nNylon-6,6 repeat unit: –NH–(CH₂)₆–NH–CO–(CH₂)₄–CO– with amide links shown and trailing bonds ✓\nSmall molecule: HCl ✓\nALLOW –CH(CH₃)–COO– for poly(lactic acid)\nALLOW (CH₂)₆ and (CH₂)₄ shown as condensed\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one repeat unit drawn\nDO NOT ALLOW H₂O for nylon-6,6 made from the diacyl dichloride` },
      { q: `A section of a polypeptide chain is shown below.\n–NH–CH(CH₃)–CO–NH–CH₂–CO–NH–CH(CH₃)–CO–NH–CH₂–CO–\n\nDraw the structures of the organic products formed when this polypeptide is hydrolysed by (i) hot aqueous hydrochloric acid and (ii) hot aqueous sodium hydroxide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `(i) H₃N⁺CH(CH₃)COOH ✓\n(i) H₃N⁺CH₂COOH ✓\n(ii) H₂NCH(CH₃)COO⁻ AND H₂NCH₂COO⁻ ✓\nALLOW Cl⁻ / Na⁺ counter ions shown or omitted\nALLOW ClH₃N– for (i); –COONa for (ii)\nDO NOT ALLOW neutral amino acids for (i) or (ii)` },
      { q: `Pentanedioic acid can be made from 1,3-dibromopropane, Br(CH₂)₃Br, in two stages. The first stage forms a dinitrile.\n\nState the reagent and conditions for the first stage and write an equation for it.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `NaCN / KCN in ethanol, heat (under reflux) ✓\nBr(CH₂)₃Br + 2NaCN → NC(CH₂)₃CN + 2NaBr ✓\nALLOW 2CN⁻ → 2Br⁻ in ionic equation\nDO NOT ALLOW HCN as reagent\nDO NOT ALLOW aqueous for conditions` },
      { q: `A chemist needs both propanoic acid and propylamine and only has bromoethane, CH₃CH₂Br, as an organic starting material. Both target compounds can be made via the same intermediate.\n\nOutline the route to each target. For each step give the reagents, conditions and an equation.`, marks: 5, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Step 1: NaCN in ethanol, heat under reflux ✓\nCH₃CH₂Br + CN⁻ → CH₃CH₂CN + Br⁻ ✓\nPropanoic acid: heat (reflux) propanenitrile with dilute HCl(aq) ✓\nCH₃CH₂CN + 2H₂O + HCl → CH₃CH₂COOH + NH₄Cl ✓\nPropylamine: H₂ with Ni catalyst AND CH₃CH₂CN + 2H₂ → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ ✓\nALLOW NaCN in equation (NaBr as product)\nALLOW H⁺ / dilute H₂SO₄ for hydrolysis with correct ammonium product\nALLOW CH₃CH₂CN + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ only if reducing agent is stated\nIGNORE 'acidified' with H₂/Ni` },
      { q: `A student prepares aspirin by warming 2-hydroxybenzoic acid with excess ethanoic anhydride and then recrystallising the product.\nC₇H₆O₃ + (CH₃CO)₂O → C₉H₈O₄ + CH₃COOH\n2-hydroxybenzoic acid, M = 138.0 g mol⁻¹; aspirin, M = 180.0 g mol⁻¹\nMass of 2-hydroxybenzoic acid used = 3.00 g\nMass of dry, recrystallised aspirin = 2.71 g\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of aspirin. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. Suggest one reason, other than an incomplete reaction, why the yield is less than 100%.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `n(2-hydroxybenzoic acid) = 3.00/138.0 = 0.02174 mol ✓\nTheoretical mass of aspirin = 0.02174 × 180.0 = 3.91 g ✓\nPercentage yield = 2.71/3.913 × 100 = 69.3% ✓\nSome aspirin remains dissolved in the (cold) solvent during recrystallisation / product lost on transfer or filtration ✓\nALLOW 69.2–69.3%\nECF from incorrect moles\nCorrect answer with no working scores first three marks\nIGNORE 'side reactions' unless a specific loss is described\nDO NOT ALLOW 'reaction reversible'` },
      { q: `Methyl 3-nitrobenzoate is made by adding a mixture of concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids to methyl benzoate, keeping the temperature below 10 °C. The mixture is then poured onto crushed ice and a crude yellow solid forms. The data book melting point of methyl 3-nitrobenzoate is 78 °C.\n\nDescribe how the student should obtain a pure, dry sample of methyl 3-nitrobenzoate from the crude solid and how the student could check its purity.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Isolating the crude product\n• Filter under reduced pressure using a Büchner / Hirsch funnel and flask\n• Wash the solid with a little ice-cold water to remove acid\n• Suck dry on the filter\n2. Recrystallisation\n• Dissolve the solid in the minimum volume of hot solvent (e.g. ethanol)\n• Filter hot if insoluble impurities are present\n• Cool slowly then in an ice bath so that crystals form; soluble impurities stay in solution\n• Filter under reduced pressure; wash crystals with a little ice-cold solvent\n• Dry between filter papers / in a desiccator / low-temperature oven\n3. Checking purity\n• Measure the melting point using a melting point apparatus / Thiele tube with the solid in a capillary tube, heating slowly near the melting point\n• Pure sample melts sharply (over a narrow range, about 1 °C) close to 78 °C\n• Impurities lower the melting point and broaden the melting range\n• ALLOW TLC: a pure sample gives a single spot\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `2-Hydroxy-2-phenylpropanoic acid, C₆H₅C(CH₃)(OH)COOH, can be made from benzene in three steps.\n\nDescribe the synthesis. For each step, give the reagents and conditions, the structure of the organic product and an equation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Step 1 – Friedel–Crafts acylation\n• Ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl, with AlCl₃ catalyst, anhydrous conditions, (reflux)\n• Product: phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃\n• C₆H₆ + CH₃COCl → C₆H₅COCH₃ + HCl\n2. Step 2 – nucleophilic addition of HCN\n• NaCN(aq) and H₂SO₄(aq) / H⁺\n• Product: 2-hydroxy-2-phenylpropanenitrile, C₆H₅C(CH₃)(OH)CN\n• C₆H₅COCH₃ + HCN → C₆H₅C(CH₃)(OH)CN\n3. Step 3 – hydrolysis of the nitrile\n• Heat under reflux with dilute HCl(aq) / dilute acid\n• C₆H₅C(CH₃)(OH)CN + 2H₂O + HCl → C₆H₅C(CH₃)(OH)COOH + NH₄Cl\n• ALLOW: product is a racemic mixture as ⁻CN attacks the planar C=O from either side\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `A student uses thin-layer chromatography (TLC) to analyse a sample of impure aspirin. The student draws the baseline in pencil and places the plate in a beaker containing solvent so that the solvent level is below the baseline.\n\nExplain why the student uses pencil for the baseline and why the solvent level is below the baseline.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Pencil (graphite) does not dissolve in the solvent / ink would dissolve and separate, giving extra spots ✓\nIf the solvent was above the baseline the spots would dissolve into the solvent instead of moving up the plate ✓\nIGNORE 'so it can be seen' for M1` },
      { q: `A TLC plate of an unknown mixture is developed. The solvent front travels 8.0 cm from the baseline. The mixture gives two spots, at 2.4 cm and 5.6 cm from the baseline. Under the same conditions, three reference compounds have these Rf values (data written for this practice question):\n| Compound | Rf |\n| A | 0.30 |\n| B | 0.55 |\n| C | 0.70 |\n\nCalculate the Rf values of the two spots and identify the compounds present in the mixture.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 2.4/8.0 = 0.30 ✓\nRf = 5.6/8.0 = 0.70 ✓\nMixture contains A and C ✓\nALLOW 0.3 and 0.7\nECF for identification from incorrect Rf values` },
      { q: `A sample of solvent is a mixture of three esters. It is analysed by gas chromatography. Assume the detector responds equally to each ester (data written for this practice question).\n| Peak | Retention time / min | Peak area / arbitrary units |\n| 1 | 2.1 | 1240 |\n| 2 | 3.4 | 860 |\n| 3 | 5.0 | 410 |\nThe esters are ethyl ethanoate (b.p. 77 °C), propyl ethanoate (b.p. 102 °C) and butyl ethanoate (b.p. 126 °C).\n\nIdentify the ester responsible for peak 1, explaining your answer, and calculate the percentage of propyl ethanoate in the mixture. Give your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Peak 1 is ethyl ethanoate ✓\nIt has the lowest boiling point / weakest London forces so it spends least time in (is least soluble in / least retained by) the stationary phase ✓\nTotal area = 1240 + 860 + 410 = 2510 ✓\n% propyl ethanoate = 860/2510 × 100 = 34.3% ✓\nALLOW 'smallest molecule' with reference to interaction with stationary phase for M2\nECF if propyl ethanoate wrongly assigned to another peak\nCorrect answer with no working scores M3 and M4` },
      { q: `A student carries out tests on four unlabelled organic liquids, A–D. The liquids are propanal, propanone, propanoic acid and propan-2-ol (data written for this practice question).\n| Test | A | B | C | D |\n| 2,4-DNP | orange precipitate | no change | orange precipitate | no change |\n| Tollens' reagent, warm | no change | no change | silver mirror | no change |\n| acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, warm | stays orange | turns green | turns green | stays orange |\n| Na₂CO₃(aq) | no change | no change | no change | effervescence |\n\nIdentify A–D, giving evidence for each, and write an equation for the reaction that causes effervescence.`, marks: 5, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `A is propanone: carbonyl (2,4-DNP positive) but not oxidised (no Tollens'/dichromate reaction) ✓\nB is propan-2-ol: oxidised by dichromate but no carbonyl group (2,4-DNP negative) ✓\nC is propanal: carbonyl AND oxidised by Tollens' (aldehyde) ✓\nD is propanoic acid: reacts with carbonate (acid) ✓\n2CH₃CH₂COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃CH₂COONa + CO₂ + H₂O ✓\nALLOW CH₃CH₂COOH + NaHCO₃ type equation only if carbonate balanced\nIdentification without any evidence: max 2 for M1–M4` },
      { q: `Samples for ¹H NMR spectroscopy are dissolved in CDCl₃ and a small amount of tetramethylsilane, Si(CH₃)₄ (TMS), is added.\n\nState why TMS is added.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `As a standard / reference for chemical shift (δ = 0 ppm) ✓\nALLOW 'to calibrate the spectrum'\nIGNORE 'gives a single peak' / 'inert' unless linked to reference` },
      { q: `Samples for ¹H NMR spectroscopy are usually dissolved in CDCl₃ rather than CHCl₃.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CDCl₃ has no ¹H atoms so it does not give a peak in the ¹H NMR spectrum (which would interfere) ✓\nALLOW 'deuterium does not produce a signal in the range of the spectrum'` },
      { q: `The ¹H NMR spectrum of 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH, has a peak at δ = 1.3 ppm (9H) and a peak at δ = 2.0 ppm (1H). A few drops of D₂O are added and the spectrum is run again.\n\nState how the second spectrum differs from the first.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `The peak at δ = 2.0 ppm (1H, O–H) disappears ✓\nALLOW 'O–H peak disappears' without the δ value\nDO NOT ALLOW 'the 1.3 ppm peak disappears'` },
      { q: `1,4-Dimethylbenzene, CH₃C₆H₄CH₃, is used to make the monomer for PET.\n\nPredict the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of 1,4-dimethylbenzene.`, marks: 1, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `3 ✓` },
      { q: `Four structural isomers of C₄H₁₀O are alcohols: butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol.\n\nPredict the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of each isomer and deduce which isomers can be identified from the number of peaks alone.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `butan-1-ol 4 AND butan-2-ol 4 ✓\n2-methylpropan-1-ol 3 AND 2-methylpropan-2-ol 2 ✓\n2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol can be identified; butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol cannot be distinguished by the number of peaks ✓\nECF for M3 from incorrect numbers of peaks` },
      { q: `Compound W, C₃H₆O₂, is one of propanoic acid, methyl ethanoate or ethyl methanoate. Its ¹H NMR spectrum is summarised below (data written for this practice question).\n| δ / ppm | Relative peak area | Splitting |\n| 11.7 | 1 | singlet |\n| 2.4 | 2 | quartet |\n| 1.2 | 3 | triplet |\n\nAnalyse the spectrum to identify W. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `δ 11.7 (1H, singlet) is the H of a COOH group ✓\nδ 2.4 (2H) is CH₂ next to C=O; quartet so adjacent to a CH₃ (3H, n+1 = 4) ✓\nδ 1.2 (3H) triplet: CH₃ adjacent to a CH₂ (2H, n+1 = 3) ✓\nW is propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH ✓\nALLOW 'O–H of carboxylic acid' for M1\nALLOW CH₂CH₃ / ethyl group for M2 and M3 combined if both splittings explained\nAn identification with no reasoning scores M4 only` },
      { q: `Compound X is a sweet-smelling liquid used as a solvent in nail varnish remover. X contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen only (data written for this practice question).\nComposition by mass: C 54.5%; H 9.1%; O 36.4%\nMass spectrum: molecular ion peak at m/z = 88; large peak at m/z = 43\nIR spectrum: strong absorption at 1740 cm⁻¹; absorption at 1240 cm⁻¹; no broad absorption between 2500 and 3500 cm⁻¹\n¹H NMR spectrum:\n| δ / ppm | Relative peak area | Splitting |\n| 4.1 | 2 | quartet |\n| 2.0 | 3 | singlet |\n| 1.3 | 3 | triplet |\n\nUse all of the information to deduce the structure of X. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 6, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Formula from composition and mass spectrum\n• C : H : O = 54.5/12.0 : 9.1/1.0 : 36.4/16.0 = 4.54 : 9.1 : 2.28 = 2 : 4 : 1\n• Empirical formula C₂H₄O (Mr = 44.0)\n• M⁺ at 88 so molecular formula C₄H₈O₂\n• m/z 43 is CH₃CO⁺\n2. IR spectrum\n• 1740 cm⁻¹ is C=O\n• 1240 cm⁻¹ is C–O\n• No broad O–H absorption so not a carboxylic acid; X is an ester\n3. ¹H NMR spectrum\n• δ 4.1 (2H) is O–CH₂; quartet so adjacent to CH₃\n• δ 2.0 (3H) singlet is CH₃ next to C=O with no adjacent H\n• δ 1.3 (3H) triplet is CH₃ adjacent to CH₂\n• X is ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃\n• ALLOW: methyl propanoate rejected as its OCH₃ would be a singlet near 3.7 ppm\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Compound Z is a colourless liquid with molecular formula C₅H₁₀O (data written for this practice question).\nTests: 2,4-DNP gives an orange precipitate; warming with Tollens' reagent gives no change; warming with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ gives no colour change.\nMass spectrum: molecular ion peak at m/z = 86\nIR spectrum: strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹; no broad absorption between 3200 and 3600 cm⁻¹\n¹³C NMR spectrum: four peaks, at δ = 212, 42, 28 and 18 ppm\n¹H NMR spectrum:\n| δ / ppm | Relative peak area | Splitting |\n| 2.6 | 1 | septet |\n| 2.1 | 3 | singlet |\n| 1.1 | 6 | doublet |\n\nUse all of the information to identify Z. Explain your reasoning.`, marks: 6, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Level of response question (6*)\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n1. Chemical tests\n• 2,4-DNP positive so Z contains a carbonyl group (aldehyde or ketone)\n• No reaction with Tollens' / no oxidation by dichromate so Z is a ketone, not an aldehyde\n2. Mass spectrum and IR\n• M⁺ = 86 confirms C₅H₁₀O (Mr = 86.0)\n• 1715 cm⁻¹ is C=O; no O–H so not an alcohol\n• ALLOW m/z 43 (CH₃CO⁺) if mentioned\n3. NMR spectra\n• Four carbon environments; δ 212 is C=O of a ketone\n• Pentan-3-one (3 environments) and pentan-2-one (5 environments) are ruled out\n• δ 2.1 (3H, singlet) is CH₃C=O with no H on the adjacent carbon\n• δ 1.1 (6H, doublet) is two equivalent CH₃ groups next to one H (CH)\n• δ 2.6 (1H, septet) is CH adjacent to 6 H (n+1 = 7), next to C=O\n• Z is 3-methylbutan-2-one, CH₃COCH(CH₃)₂\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A comprehensive response that addresses all strands in detail.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Addresses all strands but with some omissions, OR two strands in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Addresses two strands simply, OR one strand in detail.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.` }
    ]
  }
};
